      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/peritatge-contradictori-asseguranca-tercer-perit-art-38-lcs/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How the insurance contradictory appraisal and the appointment of a third expert work under article 38 of the Spanish Insurance Contract Act. Deadlines, cost and the role of the party-appointed expert.",
    "excerpt": "When the insurer's expert undervalues the damage, article 38 LCS opens a defined route: the contradictory appraisal and, if disagreement persists, a third expert.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-14",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-14",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Seguros",
    "tags": [
      "Contradictory appraisal",
      "Article 38 LCS",
      "Third expert",
      "Insurer"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Peritación contradictoria y tercer perito: qué hacer si no acepta el peritaje del seguro (art. 38 LCS)",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Counter-Report",
        "url": "/contrainforme-pericial/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When the amount offered by the insurer does not cover the real damage, the policyholder is not obliged to accept it. **Article 38 of the Spanish Insurance Contract Act (LCS, Law 50/1980)** sets out a specific procedure to resolve disputes over the amount: the **contradictory appraisal**.\n\n## What the contradictory appraisal is\n\nEach party appoints an expert. If both experts reach agreement, it binds policyholder and insurer and is recorded in a joint report. If **there is no agreement**, a **third expert** is appointed by mutual consent (or, failing that, through the courts). The report of the three —or of the third— determines the indemnity.\n\n## Deadlines you should not let slip\n\nArticle 38 sets a strict timeframe. From the moment disagreement is notified, each party has a period to appoint its expert; if the policyholder, when required by the insurer, fails to appoint one in time, they will be bound by the insurer's expert. So, faced with an insufficient offer, **appointing a party expert should not be delayed**.\n\n## What a party-appointed expert adds\n\nThe party expert does not merely put forward a different figure. They document the origin and extent of the damage with a verifiable methodology, contrast the items undervalued or excluded by the insurer and set a defensible valuation. In construction losses —fire, water damage, structural pathologies— the difference is usually substantial.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Who pays for the third expert?**\nThe third expert's fees and the costs of the contradictory appraisal are shared under article 38 LCS: each party pays its own expert, and the third expert's costs are distributed according to the outcome.\n\n**Can I also use my report in court?**\nYes. If the article 38 procedure does not resolve the dispute, the party report is expert evidence in court proceedings under the Civil Procedure Act.\n\n**Is it the same as claiming directly?**\nNo. It is the route provided in the policy itself for disputes over the amount. Exhausting it properly strengthens the policyholder's position."
  },
  {
    "title": "How to claim water damage from your insurer with an independent expert report",
    "slug": "claim-water-damage-insurance-expert-report",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/reclamar-danos-por-agua-seguro-informe-pericial/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/claim-water-damage-insurance-expert-report/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/reclamar-danys-per-aigua-asseguranca-informe-pericial/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How to claim water damage from your home or building insurance when the indemnity offered is insufficient. The role of the independent expert report and of establishing the origin of the damage.",
    "excerpt": "Water damage is the most frequent — and most disputed — loss. An independent expert report establishes the origin and stops the insurer shifting responsibility.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-21",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-21",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Seguros",
    "tags": [
      "Water damage",
      "Home insurance",
      "Claim",
      "Damp"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Cómo reclamar daños por agua a tu seguro con un informe pericial independiente",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Moisture and Water Leakage Report",
        "url": "/humedades-filtraciones/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "Water damage is the most common loss in home and building insurance, and also the one that generates the most disputes. The reason is almost always the same: **the insurer disputes the origin of the damage**, because who pays and what the policy covers depend on it.\n\n## The origin determines the cover\n\nA leak from a communal roof, a broken downpipe, structural condensation or rising damp are not the same thing. Each origin has a responsible party and a different fit within the policy. An appraisal that does not correctly identify the origin usually results in a partial indemnity or a refusal of cover.\n\n## What an independent expert report establishes\n\nAn independent technical report locates the origin of the water with **non-destructive methodology** —infrared thermography, depth hygrometers, watertightness tests— without relying on the insurer's version. From there it quantifies the damage and delimits liability. That document is the basis for negotiating a fair indemnity or, if the offer remains insufficient, for triggering the contradictory appraisal under article 38 LCS.\n\n## Repair is a later stage\n\nTwo stages should not be confused. The expert report establishes the origin, extent and liability —the **evidence** stage. The **repair** of the damage (waterproofing, treatment) is a separate stage carried out by a specialist contractor. Damp treatment in Barcelona has specific solutions that fall outside the scope of the expert report.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Does home insurance cover leaks?**\nIt depends on the origin and the policy terms. An accidental leak is usually covered; rising damp or a pre-existing construction defect often is not. The expert report is what distinguishes one case from the other.\n\n**Who is liable if the leak comes from the communal roof?**\nIf the origin is in a common element, liability falls on the homeowners' association and its insurance, not on the affected owner.\n\n**And if the insurer offers less than the repair costs?**\nYou can reject the offer and request the contradictory appraisal (art. 38 LCS), providing your own report."
  },
  {
    "title": "Assessing an industrial warehouse fire: cause, origin and insurance claim",
    "slug": "industrial-warehouse-fire-assessment-cause-origin-rscei",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/peritaje-incendio-nave-industrial-causa-origen-rscei/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/industrial-warehouse-fire-assessment-cause-origin-rscei/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/peritatge-incendi-nau-industrial-causa-origen-rscei/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How fire damage to an industrial warehouse is assessed: cause and origin determination, steel structure evaluation, RSCIEI compliance and the claim to the insurer.",
    "excerpt": "An industrial fire combines two disputes at once: the cause and origin of the loss and the extent of the damage. Both condition the indemnity and liability.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-28",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-28",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Naves Industriales",
    "tags": [
      "Industrial fire",
      "RSCIEI",
      "Steel structure",
      "Insurance assessment"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Peritaje tras un incendio en nave industrial: causa, origen y reclamación al seguro",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Industrial Warehouse Assessment",
        "url": "/naves-industriales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "A fire in a logistics or industrial warehouse concentrates high-value losses and complex technical disputes. The assessment must resolve two questions the insurer will scrutinise: **the cause and origin** of the fire, and **the real extent of the damage** to the structure and the building.\n\n## Cause and origin: the starting point\n\nDetermining cause and origin conditions cover. An electrical fault, negligent use, an installation defect or an external cause are not the same. The analysis relies on the damage pattern, the areas of highest temperature and the traceability of the fire, and must be consistent with the loss documentation.\n\n## Damage to the steel structure\n\nHeat degrades steel well before melting it: loss of strength in beams and purlins, weld fatigue and roof deformation require assessing whether the structure retains load-bearing capacity or needs replacement. This assessment is decisive in the valuation, because it marks the difference between repairing and rebuilding.\n\n## RSCIEI compliance\n\nThe **Regulation on Fire Safety in Industrial Establishments (RSCIEI, RD 2267/2004)** sets the conditions the warehouse had to meet. Checking compliance at the time of the loss is a routine part of the report, because a prior breach may affect cover or the allocation of liability.\n\n## Claim and contradictory appraisal\n\nWhen the insurer's offer does not cover the proven damage, the policyholder can trigger the contradictory appraisal under article 38 LCS by providing their own report. In industrial losses, the valuation gap usually more than justifies the procedure.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**What should be done in the first hours after an industrial fire?**\nPreserve the scene as far as possible, document the state with photographs and do not remove debris until the cause-and-origin inspection has been carried out.\n\n**Is a structure that survived the fire safe?**\nNot necessarily. Steel may have lost load-bearing capacity without collapsing. It requires technical assessment before the warehouse is reused.\n\n**Can I claim for loss of profits?**\nBusiness-interruption cover depends on the policy. The report documents the time and works needed for reinstatement, the basis for that claim."
  },
  {
    "title": "Damage to a warehouse at the end of the lease: normal wear versus claimable damage",
    "slug": "warehouse-damage-end-of-lease-normal-wear-claim",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/danos-nave-industrial-fin-alquiler-uso-normal-reclamacion/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/warehouse-damage-end-of-lease-normal-wear-claim/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/danys-nau-industrial-fi-lloguer-us-normal-reclamacio/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How to distinguish normal wear and tear from damage attributable to the tenant at the end of an industrial warehouse lease: flooring, structure, installations and unauthorised modifications.",
    "excerpt": "When returning an industrial warehouse, the line between normal wear and tenant-attributable damage decides who bears the repair. An objective report draws that line.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-08-04",
    "dateModified": "2026-08-04",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Naves Industriales",
    "tags": [
      "End-of-lease damage",
      "Lease",
      "Industrial flooring",
      "Condition"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Daños en una nave al finalizar el alquiler: qué es uso normal y qué se puede reclamar",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Industrial Warehouse Assessment",
        "url": "/naves-industriales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "At the end of a logistics or industrial warehouse lease, landlord and tenant usually disagree about the condition on return. The underlying question is always the same: **what corresponds to normal wear from the agreed use and what constitutes damage attributable to the tenant**.\n\n## The line between normal wear and damage\n\nOrdinary use of a warehouse produces expected wear. Attributable damage, by contrast, stems from improper use, unforeseen overloads or unauthorised modifications. The report contrasts the return condition with the initial condition, the lease and the foreseen use loads.\n\n## The usual points of conflict\n\n**Logistics flooring** concentrates much of the dispute: cracking from overload, joint deterioration, punching from racking or forklifts. So do modifications to the structure or installations —drilling, anchoring, changes to fire protection— and damage to loading docks and cladding.\n\n## Why an objective report matters\n\nWithout a technical document, the negotiation becomes a clash of perceptions. The expert report sets out, with verifiable criteria, what damage exists, its cause and the cost to remedy it, separating the claimable from simple wear. That document serves both for negotiation and for enforcing the deposit or the court claim.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can the landlord withhold the deposit for normal wear?**\nNo. The deposit covers damage attributable to the tenant, not the deterioration inherent to the agreed use. Distinguishing one from the other is precisely the purpose of the report.\n\n**Is the initial inventory useful?**\nVery much so. The condition documented on entry is the reference against which the exit condition is assessed.\n\n**Who commissions the assessment?**\nEither party can commission it as a party report. A rigorous report usually makes agreement possible without going to court."
  },
  {
    "title": "Variations and cost overruns in public works: the expert report in LCSP contracts",
    "slug": "claim-variations-cost-overruns-public-works-lcsp",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/reclamar-modificados-sobrecostes-obra-publica-lcsp-informe-pericial/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/claim-variations-cost-overruns-public-works-lcsp/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/reclamar-modificats-sobrecostos-obra-publica-lcsp-informe-pericial/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How to substantiate claims for project variations, cost overruns and contract settlement in public works through an expert report under the Spanish Public Sector Contracts Act.",
    "excerpt": "In public procurement, unrecognised overruns and variations are claimed with technical support. The expert report translates the executed works into the language of the contract and the LCSP.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-08-11",
    "dateModified": "2026-08-11",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Obra Pública",
    "tags": [
      "LCSP",
      "Project variations",
      "Cost overruns",
      "Contract settlement"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Modificados y sobrecostes en obra pública: el informe pericial en contratos LCSP",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Public Works Assessment",
        "url": "/obras-publicas/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "In public works, the gap between what was designed and what was actually built produces some of the highest-value claims. Unrecognised variations, unforeseen work items, cost overruns from supervening circumstances or disputes over contract settlement: all require **verifiable technical support** to succeed against the public authority.\n\n## The framework: the Public Sector Contracts Act\n\nThe **LCSP** governs contract modifications, price review and settlement. A solid claim does not argue in the abstract: it contrasts the executed works with the project, the specifications and the contractual rules, and fits each item into the corresponding legal provision. The expert report is the instrument that makes that translation.\n\n## What the report documents\n\nThe assessment measures and values what was actually built, identifies unforeseen or modified items, analyses the causes —design errors, ground conditions, instructions from the site management— and quantifies the overrun with objective criteria. In contract settlement, it delimits the outstanding items and the disputed penalties.\n\n## Why rigour is decisive here\n\nAgainst a public authority, the burden of technically justifying the claim falls on the contractor. A report documenting each item with measurements, contractual references and calculation is what sustains the claim in the administrative phase and, where applicable, before the administrative courts.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can you claim a variation already executed but not formalised?**\nThis is a frequent and complex situation. The report documents the reality of the executed works, the factual basis of the claim, without prejudice to the lawyer's legal analysis.\n\n**What is the difference between a variation and complementary works?**\nThey fit differently under the LCSP. The expert report provides the technical data to classify each action correctly.\n\n**Is it useful for contract settlement?**\nYes. Settlement is one of the moments where expert support is most useful to fix the real balance between the parties."
  },
  {
    "title": "New-build defects: how to claim against the developer under the LOE (1, 3 and 10-year periods)",
    "slug": "new-build-defects-claim-developer-loe-deadlines",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/defectos-obra-nueva-reclamar-promotora-loe-plazos/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/new-build-defects-claim-developer-loe-deadlines/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/defectes-obra-nova-reclamar-promotora-loe-terminis/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Which defects the Spanish Building Act covers, the 1, 3 and 10-year warranty periods, and how an expert report proves the defect and liability against developer and builder.",
    "excerpt": "The LOE sets three warranty tiers by the seriousness of the defect. Knowing which one your problem fits —and proving it in time— decides a new-build claim.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-08-18",
    "dateModified": "2026-08-18",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "LOE",
      "New build",
      "Construction defects",
      "Ten-year warranty"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Defectos en obra nueva: cómo reclamar a la promotora con la LOE (plazos de 1, 3 y 10 años)",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When defects appear in a new-build home or development, the route to claim is set by the **Spanish Building Act (LOE, Law 38/1999)**. Its article 17 organises liability into three warranty tiers according to the seriousness of the defect, each with its own period.\n\n## The three LOE periods\n\n**One year** for finishing defects. **Three years** for defects affecting habitability —damp, insulation, installations—. **Ten years** for damage compromising the stability and structural safety of the building. The periods run from the handover of the works, and are warranty periods: the defect must appear within the corresponding period.\n\n## Who is liable\n\nThe LOE distinguishes between developer, builder, designer and site management. Liability may be individual or joint depending on whether the cause can be individualised. Determining the origin of the defect —design, management or execution— is precisely what allows the claim to be directed at the right party.\n\n## The role of the expert report\n\nThe report establishes three things: that the defect exists, which LOE warranty tier it fits and which party is responsible. It also quantifies the cost of remedy. Without that support, the claim is a mere assertion; with it, it becomes a defensible position.\n\n## Do not confuse it with the Civil Code\n\nNew-build defects are governed by the LOE; hidden defects in a sale between private individuals, by the Civil Code, with different periods. Framing the case correctly from the outset avoids losing time limits.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**When do the periods start?**\nFrom the date of the handover certificate, not from the purchase deed.\n\n**What is the ten-year warranty?**\nThe ten-year cover for structural damage, backed by the developer's compulsory decennial insurance.\n\n**Do I have to claim before the damage appears?**\nNo. The damage must appear within the warranty period; once it does, there is also an additional period to bring the action. Acting without delay is prudent."
  },
  {
    "title": "Differential settlement in foundations: how it is diagnosed and proven",
    "slug": "differential-settlement-foundation-diagnosis-report",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/asientos-diferenciales-cimentacion-diagnostico-informe-pericial/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/differential-settlement-foundation-diagnosis-report/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/assentaments-diferencials-fonamentacio-diagnostic-informe-pericial/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What differential settlement is, how it differs from other pathologies, which cracks reveal it and how an expert report establishes its cause and extent to claim or intervene.",
    "excerpt": "Differential settlement leaves a recognisable signature in a building's cracks. Diagnosing its cause —ground, foundation or surroundings— is the step before any repair or claim.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-08-25",
    "dateModified": "2026-08-25",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Patologías Estructurales",
    "tags": [
      "Differential settlement",
      "Foundation",
      "Cracks",
      "Geotechnics"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Asientos diferenciales en la cimentación: cómo se diagnostican y se prueban",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When one part of a building settles more than another, **differential settlement** occurs: one of the most serious structural damage mechanisms and, at the same time, one that leaves the most recognisable signature on the facade and partitions.\n\n## How it is recognised\n\nDifferential settlement usually shows as **inclined cracks** following a path consistent with the ground movement, wider towards the zone that has dropped most. They are often accompanied by tilting, doors that no longer fit and uneven floors. The pattern, width and evolution of the cracks are the first diagnostic clue.\n\n## The possible causes\n\nThe origin may lie in the ground —expansive soils, poorly compacted fill, changes in moisture—, in the foundation itself —inadequate or poorly built— or in the surroundings: adjacent excavations, pipe leaks washing out fines, nearby trees. Determining the cause requires combining the pathology inspection with geotechnical analysis.\n\n## What the expert report establishes\n\nThe report determines whether the settlement is **active or stabilised**, its cause and which structural elements are compromised, with reference to the CTE DB-SE and applicable standards. This information is essential both to direct a claim and to size the intervention.\n\n## Repair is another stage\n\nStabilising settlement —by underpinning, injection or other techniques— is specialist work carried out by a technical contractor once the cause is determined. The report defines the problem; the constructive solution is designed from it.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Do all cracks indicate settlement?**\nNo. Many are thermal, shrinkage or partition cracks. Differential settlement has a characteristic pattern.\n\n**How do I know if the settlement is still active?**\nThrough monitoring with tell-tales and comparing measurements over time.\n\n**Can it affect the building's safety?**\nIn serious cases, yes. That is why early diagnosis matters."
  },
  {
    "title": "Unfavourable building inspection (ITE) in Barcelona: what it means and how to act on serious defects",
    "slug": "unfavourable-building-inspection-barcelona-serious-defects",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/ite-desfavorable-barcelona-deficiencias-graves-que-hacer/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/unfavourable-building-inspection-barcelona-serious-defects/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/ite-desfavorable-barcelona-deficiencies-greus-que-fer/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What an unfavourable Technical Building Inspection with serious defects means in Barcelona, the deadlines it opens and how to prioritise works with independent technical criteria.",
    "excerpt": "An unfavourable ITE is not the end of the process: it opens deadlines and obligations. Understanding the real extent of the defects avoids both alarm and inaction.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-09-01",
    "dateModified": "2026-09-01",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "ITE",
    "tags": [
      "ITE",
      "Technical Building Inspection",
      "Serious defects",
      "Barcelona"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "ITE desfavorable en Barcelona: qué significa y cómo actuar si hay deficiencias graves",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Structural Consulting",
        "url": "/asesoramiento-estructural/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "The **Technical Building Inspection (ITE)** is mandatory in Catalonia for buildings above a certain age. When the result is **unfavourable** or detects **serious defects**, many communities react with alarm or, conversely, with inaction. Neither helps: what is needed is to understand the real extent of what has been detected.\n\n## What an unfavourable result means\n\nAn unfavourable ITE indicates that the building has defects affecting its state of conservation that must be remedied. The rating carries deadlines and the obligation to undertake works, and can affect grants and procedures. **Serious** or **very serious** defects require priority action and, sometimes, immediate precautionary measures.\n\n## The risk of intervening without diagnosis\n\nThe ITE report describes symptoms, but does not always go into the cause or the optimal solution. Undertaking works without a prior diagnosis can lead to treating the symptom and not the origin —very common with damp and structural pathologies— and to spending on repairs that do not solve the problem.\n\n## How to prioritise with independent criteria\n\nAn independent technical analysis ranks the defects by seriousness and urgency, distinguishes the structural from the non-structural, and defines what must be tackled first and how. This lets the community plan spending and avoid both paralysis and rushed decisions.\n\n## When liability is involved\n\nIf the defects stem from construction defects still under warranty (LOE) or from a third party's action, the diagnosis may also open a route to claim. In that case the report acquires evidential value.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Which buildings must undergo the ITE in Barcelona?**\nThose exceeding the age set by Catalan regulations, with periodic renewal.\n\n**What happens if the defects are not remedied?**\nNon-compliance may lead to penalties and loss of access to grants, as well as risk to the building.\n\n**Does the ITE assess the cause of the pathologies?**\nNot in depth. Determining cause, liability and solution requires a specific study."
  },
  {
    "title": "Poorly executed renovation: how to claim with an expert report",
    "slug": "poorly-executed-renovation-claim-expert-report",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/reforma-mal-ejecutada-reclamar-informe-pericial-lex-artis/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/poorly-executed-renovation-claim-expert-report/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/reforma-mal-executada-reclamar-informe-pericial-lex-artis/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How to claim for a badly done renovation: contrasting the executed works with the budget and specification, the constructive lex artis and the role of the expert report in the claim against the contractor.",
    "excerpt": "Materials inferior to those agreed, items billed but not carried out, poor finishes. A poorly executed renovation is claimed by contrasting what was promised with what was actually done.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-09-08",
    "dateModified": "2026-09-08",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Poor renovation",
      "Lex artis",
      "Specification",
      "Claim"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Reforma mal ejecutada: cómo reclamar con un informe pericial",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "A renovation that does not match what was agreed is one of the most frequent claims among private individuals and communities. Poor finishes, materials of lower quality than agreed, items budgeted and billed but not carried out: the problem is not one of perception, it is one of **contrast between what was promised and what was actually done**.\n\n## What the works are compared against\n\nThe assessment contrasts what was executed against three references: the **contract and budget**, the **specification** and **good construction practice** or *lex artis*. This triple contrast objectifies the breaches: what was agreed, what was billed and what was actually built, and to what quality.\n\n## What the report documents\n\nThe report identifies each defect, determines its cause, values the cost of remedy and quantifies, where applicable, the items not carried out. This document is the basis of the claim, whether resolved amicably or in court.\n\n## The usual claim process\n\nIn many cases, a professional report is enough for the contractor to remedy without going to court. If there is no agreement, mediation with a technical basis is possible and, as a last resort, court proceedings, where the expert report is the key evidence. Ratification of the report before the judge is part of the service.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Do I need a report to claim for a badly done renovation?**\nIt is not compulsory in the pre-litigation phase, but it is the most effective tool to get the contractor to remedy. In court it is practically essential.\n\n**Does the LOE apply to a renovation?**\nIt depends on the scope. Renovations that do not constitute complete building works are usually governed by the contract and the Civil Code; the specific framing is a matter for legal analysis.\n\n**Can I claim if I have already paid for the renovation?**\nYes. Having paid does not prevent claiming for defects or for items not carried out."
  },
  {
    "title": "Common errors in a construction expert report (and how they are refuted)",
    "slug": "common-errors-opposing-expert-report-counter-report",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/errores-frecuentes-informe-pericial-contrario-contrainforme/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/common-errors-opposing-expert-report-counter-report/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/errors-frequents-informe-pericial-contrari-contrainforme/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "The most frequent methodological failings in the opposing party's construction reports and how a counter-report detects and rebuts them under the court's free evaluation of evidence (LEC art. 348).",
    "excerpt": "Not every expert report is sound. A counter-report does not produce another report: it shows the opposing one is incomplete, incorrect or methodologically deficient.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-09-15",
    "dateModified": "2026-09-15",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Counter-report",
      "Challenge expert report",
      "LEC 348",
      "Expert methodology"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Errores frecuentes en un informe pericial de construcción (y cómo se refutan)",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Counter-Report",
        "url": "/contrainforme-pericial/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "Not all reports submitted in proceedings withstand rigorous technical analysis. When the opposing party's report contains shortcomings, the response is not to produce another report on the pathology, but a **counter-report**: a document showing that the rival report's conclusions are incorrect, incomplete or methodologically deficient.\n\n## The most common errors\n\n**Insufficient inspection:** categorical conclusions from a superficial visit, without tests or measurements to support them. **Incorrect regulatory references** or cited out of context —CTE, EHE-08, LOE applied where they do not belong—. **Logical leap** between the data gathered and the conclusions. **Economic quantification** without justification of measurements or prices. **Confusion of causes**: attributing to one origin what belongs to another.\n\n## What the counter-report analyses\n\nThe counter-report reviews the methodological rigour of the inspection, the correctness of the regulatory references, the coherence between data and conclusions and the soundness of the valuation. It does not disqualify in the abstract: it points out, item by item, where the opposing report ceases to be defensible.\n\n## Why it works before the judge\n\nThe judge evaluates expert evidence under the **free evaluation standard (LEC art. 348)**. They are not obliged to accept a report simply because it exists. A counter-report showing concrete deficiencies diminishes the evidential weight of the opposing report.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can the judge reject an expert report?**\nThe judge does not formally reject it, but freely evaluates its evidential force. A sound counter-report reduces the credit given to the opposing report.\n\n**How long does a counter-report take?**\nIt depends on the volume of the report to be rebutted; usually between ten and twenty working days.\n\n**Is it the same as challenging the expert (tacha)?**\nNo. The tacha questions the expert's impartiality (LEC art. 343); the counter-report questions the technical content. They can be used together."
  },
  {
    "title": "Party-appointed and court-appointed expert: differences and when each is used",
    "slug": "difference-party-appointed-court-appointed-expert",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/diferencia-perito-parte-perito-judicial/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/difference-party-appointed-court-appointed-expert/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/diferencia-perit-part-perit-judicial/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Differences between the party-appointed and court-appointed expert: who appoints them, what validity they have and when each figure is appropriate in a construction dispute.",
    "excerpt": "Both produce the same type of report with identical methodology; what changes is who appoints them and at which stage of the dispute they intervene.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-09-22",
    "dateModified": "2026-09-22",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Proceso",
    "tags": [
      "Party-appointed expert",
      "Court-appointed expert",
      "LEC",
      "Appointment"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Perito de parte y perito judicial: diferencias y cuándo se usa cada uno",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Court-Appointed Expert Witness",
        "url": "/perito-judicial/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "In a construction dispute two figures are often confused: the **party-appointed expert** and the **court-appointed expert**. The report methodology is the same; what changes is **who commissions it and at which stage of the proceedings it intervenes**.\n\n## The party-appointed expert\n\nCommissioned directly by one of the parties —or its lawyer— to investigate the technical reality of the facts and support its position. It is fully valid as evidence under the **Civil Procedure Act (LEC, arts. 335 ff.)**. It is used before a claim to assess the strength of the case, during pre-litigation negotiation, or as expert evidence at trial.\n\n## The court-appointed expert\n\nAppointed by the court, usually by lot among those registered on the official Ministry of Justice list, or by agreement of the parties (**LEC art. 339**). Its distinctive feature is **accredited impartiality**. It must ratify the report orally before the judge and submit to cross-examination by both lawyers (**LEC art. 347**).\n\n## When each is appropriate\n\nThe party expert is the instrument to **build** your position: it brings initiative and control. The court expert brings the weight of institutional neutrality. In many disputes they coexist.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is a party report worth less for not being \"impartial\"?**\nNo. The LEC recognises it as expert evidence. The judge weighs its evidential force by the rigour of the work, not by who commissioned it.\n\n**Can the same expert act as party and court expert?**\nIn different proceedings, yes. In the same matter, the role is set by who appoints them.\n\n**Which is more expensive?**\nFees depend on the complexity of the case, not on the figure."
  },
  {
    "title": "What an expert report must contain under the LEC",
    "slug": "what-an-expert-report-must-contain-lec",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/que-debe-contener-informe-pericial-lec/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/what-an-expert-report-must-contain-lec/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/que-ha-de-contenir-informe-pericial-lec/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "The structure and contents a construction expert report must include to be sound under the Spanish Civil Procedure Act: object, methodology, analysis, conclusions and ratification.",
    "excerpt": "A report is not worth its length, but its traceability: that each conclusion can be followed from the data to the standard applied.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-09-29",
    "dateModified": "2026-09-29",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Proceso",
    "tags": [
      "Expert report",
      "LEC 335",
      "Methodology",
      "Report"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Qué debe contener un informe pericial conforme a la LEC",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Counter-Report",
        "url": "/contrainforme-pericial/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "A construction expert report is not measured by its length, but by its **traceability**: that each conclusion can be followed from the data to the standard applied. The **LEC (arts. 335 ff.)** does not impose a rigid format, but practice has consolidated the contents that make a report sound.\n\n## Object and methodology\n\nThe report must precisely define **what it is asked** and **what method** was used to answer: visual inspection, tests, measurements, modelling. Methodology is the first thing a counter-report will examine, so it must be explicit and reproducible.\n\n## Data, analysis and standards\n\nThe data gathered on site (cracks, damp, deformations) are documented with photographs and references. The analysis interprets them in light of the applicable standard —**CTE, EHE-08, LOE**— without logical leaps. Economic quantification is justified with measurements and prices.\n\n## Conclusions and ratification\n\nThe conclusions answer, one by one, the questions posed, with the degree of certainty the data allow. The expert must be able to **ratify** the report and defend it under cross-examination.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is a specific format compulsory?**\nNo. The LEC does not require it, but an orderly report is harder to rebut.\n\n**What distinguishes a good report from a weak one?**\nThe coherence between data and conclusions and the correctness of the regulatory references.\n\n**Does it include the repair valuation?**\nUsually yes, when the scope of the commission requires it."
  },
  {
    "title": "How to prove hidden defects: the pre-existence test",
    "slug": "how-to-prove-hidden-defects-pre-existence",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/como-demostrar-vicios-ocultos-preexistencia-perito/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/how-to-prove-hidden-defects-pre-existence/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/com-demostrar-vicis-ocults-preexistencia-perit/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How a hidden defect in a home sale is technically established: proving that the defect is serious, non-apparent and prior to the sale within the Civil Code time limit.",
    "excerpt": "The key to a hidden-defects claim is not that the defect exists, but proving it existed before the purchase. That is proven technically.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-06",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-06",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Vicios Ocultos",
    "tags": [
      "Hidden defects",
      "Pre-existence",
      "Civil Code",
      "Evidence"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Cómo demostrar vicios ocultos: la prueba de preexistencia",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Hidden Defects Report",
        "url": "/vicios-ocultos/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "In a hidden-defects claim, the defect is usually obvious once discovered. The hard —and decisive— part is proving that it **already existed before the purchase** and that it **was not apparent** on an ordinary inspection. That is where the expert report makes the difference.\n\n## What the Civil Code requires\n\nThe **Civil Code (arts. 1484 ff.)** requires the defect to be serious, non-apparent and prior to the sale. The buyer has **6 months** from delivery to bring the action (art. 1490), a limitation period running against them. The report must be obtained within that period.\n\n## How pre-existence is proven\n\nPre-existence is established by analysing the **degree of development** of the pathology. Rising damp, the evolution of a crack or the state of corrosion allow its age to be estimated: damage does not appear instantly, and its technical maturity reveals that it was already present at the time of sale.\n\n## Seriousness and non-apparency\n\nThe report also establishes that the defect **compromises the use** of the property (seriousness) and that it **was not detectable** on a normal visit.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**How long do I have to claim?**\n6 months from delivery (Civil Code art. 1490).\n\n**And if the seller says the damage is new?**\nPrecisely the analysis of the degree of development of the pathology allows that claim to be rebutted with technical criteria.\n\n**Does any defect qualify?**\nNo. It must be serious, hidden and prior to the sale."
  },
  {
    "title": "Damp in a homeowners' association: common or private element?",
    "slug": "damp-homeowners-association-common-or-private",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/humedades-comunidad-elemento-comun-privativo/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/damp-homeowners-association-common-or-private/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/humitats-comunitat-element-comu-privatiu/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How to determine whether damp in a homeowners' association comes from a common or private element, who must bear the repair and how an expert report establishes it.",
    "excerpt": "In a community, the question that decides who pays is where the water comes from. The answer comes from diagnosing the origin, not from the location of the damage.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-13",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-13",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Humedades",
    "tags": [
      "Damp",
      "Homeowners' association",
      "Common element",
      "Claim"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Humedades en comunidad: ¿elemento común o privativo?",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Moisture and Water Leakage Report",
        "url": "/humedades-filtraciones/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When damp appears in a building under commonhold, the conflict is almost never technical at first: it is about **who pays**. And that answer depends on a single question: whether the water comes from a **common element** or a **private** one.\n\n## Why the origin decides everything\n\nIf the damp comes from a common element —roof, facade, general downpipes, floor slabs— the repair falls on the **community**. If it comes from a private element —an interior installation of a flat— it falls on the **owner**. The damage may show in one flat and originate in the communal roof: that is why the location of the stain is not enough.\n\n## Diagnosing the origin\n\nLocating the origin requires non-destructive methodology: **infrared thermography**, depth hygrometers, watertightness tests. The report determines the exact point of water entry and its path, and thereby assigns liability under the Commonhold Act.\n\n## Repair comes later\n\nOnce the origin and liability are determined, the repair is carried out by a specialist contractor; damp treatment in Barcelona has specific solutions that fall outside the scope of the report.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Who pays if the leak comes from the roof?**\nThe roof is a common element: the community and, where applicable, its insurance respond.\n\n**And if the origin is in a neighbour's private-use terrace?**\nTerraces are usually common elements for private use; liability is shared according to the title and the cause. The report delimits it.\n\n**Can I claim against the community with a party report?**\nYes. It is the usual tool to substantiate the claim at the general meeting or in court."
  },
  {
    "title": "Facade leaks: origin, liability and claim",
    "slug": "facade-leaks-origin-liability-claim",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/filtraciones-fachada-origen-responsabilidad-reclamacion/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/facade-leaks-origin-liability-claim/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/filtracions-facana-origen-responsabilitat-reclamacio/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Causes of facade leaks (joints, windows, cracks, ventilated facade), how the origin is diagnosed and how liability is delimited to claim.",
    "excerpt": "The facade is one of the hardest water-entry routes to diagnose: the entry point and the point where the damage appears rarely coincide.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-20",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-20",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Humedades",
    "tags": [
      "Leaks",
      "Facade",
      "CTE DB-HS",
      "Claim"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Filtraciones por fachada: origen, responsabilidad y reclamación",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Moisture and Water Leakage Report",
        "url": "/humedades-filtraciones/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "Facade leaks are among the hardest damp pathologies to diagnose: water enters at one point and appears at another, after travelling inside the enclosure. Without a rigorous diagnosis, repairs pile up without solving the problem.\n\n## The usual causes\n\nDegraded **joints and seals**, poorly fitted **windows** without a water barrier, **cracks** in the render, defects in **ventilated facades** or at junctions with the roof and balconies. **CTE DB-HS 1** sets the watertightness requirements; its breach is usually at the origin of the pathology.\n\n## How the origin is located\n\nDiagnosis combines inspection of the construction detail, **thermography** and targeted watertightness tests that reproduce water entry in a controlled way. The aim is to identify the real point of entry, not the visible symptom.\n\n## Liability and claim\n\nOnce the origin is determined, the report delimits liability: builder (execution defect within the LOE period), community (common element) or insurance cover. The subsequent repair is a matter for a specialist contractor.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Why have previous repairs failed?**\nBecause the symptom (the stain) was probably treated without identifying the point of water entry.\n\n**Does insurance cover facade leaks?**\nIt depends on the origin and the policy. A pre-existing construction defect usually falls outside cover.\n\n**Does it fall within the LOE warranty?**\nWatertightness affects habitability: 3-year warranty (LOE art. 17)."
  },
  {
    "title": "Concrete carbonation: expert diagnosis (it is not aluminosis)",
    "slug": "concrete-carbonation-expert-diagnosis",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/carbonatacion-hormigon-diagnostico-pericial/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/concrete-carbonation-expert-diagnosis/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/carbonatacio-formigo-diagnostic-pericial/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What concrete carbonation is, how it is diagnosed with the phenolphthalein test, how it differs from aluminosis and the extent of the structural damage.",
    "excerpt": "Carbonation and aluminosis are often confused, but they are different phenomena with different causes and prognoses. Telling them apart changes the claim.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-27",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-27",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Patologías Estructurales",
    "tags": [
      "Carbonation",
      "Concrete",
      "Corrosion",
      "Diagnosis"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Carbonatación del hormigón: diagnóstico pericial (no es aluminosis)",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When rusted rebar appears in columns, slabs or beams, \"aluminosis\" is quickly mentioned. But often the phenomenon is **carbonation**, a different process worth diagnosing correctly because it changes the cause, the prognosis and the claim.\n\n## What carbonation is\n\nConcrete protects the reinforcement because its environment is alkaline. Over time, ambient **CO₂** penetrates and **reduces the alkalinity** (it \"carbonates\" it); when the carbonation front reaches the reinforcement, it loses its protection and begins to **corrode**. Corrosion expands the steel, cracks the concrete and causes spalling.\n\n## How it is diagnosed\n\nThe reference test is the **phenolphthalein** test: applied to a fresh fracture of the concrete, it stains the sound (alkaline) zone pink and leaves the carbonated one colourless. Combined with measuring the depth of the front and the real cover of the reinforcement, it allows the extent to be estimated.\n\n## Difference from aluminosis\n\n**Aluminosis** derives from the use of high-alumina cement (common in 1950s–70s slabs); it is detected with specific tests. Carbonation affects any concrete through ageing. The differential diagnosis is essential.\n\nRepair (passivation, reinforcement) is a later stage carried out by a specialist technical contractor.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is carbonation dangerous?**\nIt depends on the extent of corrosion and the reinforcement section lost. The report assesses the residual load-bearing capacity.\n\n**Can it be stopped?**\nRepair seeks to restore the reinforcement's protection. Feasibility is determined by the diagnosis.\n\n**Do aluminosis or carbonation trigger the ten-year warranty?**\nIt depends on the building's age and the origin; in new builds, a structural defect fits the LOE ten-year warranty."
  },
