      }
    ],
    "content": "Rebar corrosion is the most frequent pathology of aged reinforced concrete, and also one of the worst assessed: visible rust is only the symptom. What matters is **how much steel section has been lost** and what it means for the structure.\n\n## What causes it\n\nTwo main causes: **carbonation** of the concrete (loss of alkalinity through CO₂) and the presence of **chlorides** (marine environments, de-icing salts). Both break the steel's protective layer and activate corrosion.\n\n## How the extent is measured\n\nDiagnosis determines the **carbonation depth** or chloride content, the **real cover** and, above all, the **section loss** of the bars. With that figure the residual load-bearing capacity is assessed under the structural standards (EHE-08 / Eurocode 2).\n\n## What the report adds\n\nThe report distinguishes a **cosmetic or superficial** problem from one that **affects stability**, and sets the urgency of the intervention. Repair or reinforcement is carried out by a specialist contractor.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Does visible rust mean immediate danger?**\nNot necessarily. It may be superficial or indicate significant section loss. Only measurement determines it.\n\n**Can it be repaired without replacing the structure?**\nIn many cases yes, through cleaning, passivation and reinforcement. Feasibility is set by the diagnosis.\n\n**Who is liable in a recent building?**\nA structural defect in a new build fits the LOE ten-year warranty."
  },
  {
    "title": "Crack or fissure: how to tell them apart and when to worry",
    "slug": "crack-or-fissure-difference-when-to-worry",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/fisura-o-grieta-diferencia-cuando-preocupan/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/crack-or-fissure-difference-when-to-worry/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/fissura-o-esquerda-diferencia-quan-preocupen/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "The difference between a fissure and a crack, how to read its path, width and evolution, and when a crack indicates a structural problem requiring an expert report.",
    "excerpt": "Not every crack is structural, but some are. The path, the width and the evolution are the three clues that separate the cosmetic from the serious.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-11-10",
    "dateModified": "2026-11-10",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Patologías Estructurales",
    "tags": [
      "Fissures",
      "Cracks",
      "Structural pathology",
      "Diagnosis"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Fisura o grieta: cómo se diferencian y cuándo preocupan",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "A crack at home causes alarm, but not all indicate a structural problem. Telling a superficial **fissure** from a **crack** that compromises the structure is the first step, and it depends on reading three signals correctly.\n\n## Fissure versus crack\n\nAs a guide, a **fissure** has a fine opening affecting the finish, and a **crack** is wider and passes through the element. But width alone is not enough: the decisive factor is the **origin**.\n\n## The three clues\n\n**Path:** structural cracks usually follow a path consistent with the damage mechanism. **Width:** above a certain threshold and with a diagonal layout, structural suspicion rises. **Evolution:** an **active** crack, growing over time, is more worrying than a stabilised one.\n\n## Other causes\n\nMany fissures are **thermal**, **shrinkage** or **partition** cracks, with no structural significance. The report distinguishes the origin before attributing seriousness.\n\n## When a report is advisable\n\nWhen the crack is active, affects load-bearing elements, exceeds a millimetre with a diagonal layout, or is accompanied by deformation.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is a crack that does not grow safe?**\nA stabilised crack is less worrying than an active one, but confirming its origin is advisable.\n\n**How do I know if it is active?**\nThrough tell-tales and measurements repeated over time.\n\n**Can I just fill the crack?**\nFilling without diagnosis hides the symptom. If the origin is structural, it will reappear."
  },
  {
    "title": "Industrial flooring: TR-34 tolerances and flatness disputes",
    "slug": "industrial-flooring-tr34-tolerances-disputes",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/pavimento-industrial-tolerancias-tr34-disputas/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/industrial-flooring-tr34-tolerances-disputes/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/paviment-industrial-toleracies-tr34-disputes/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How defects in industrial flooring are assessed: cracking, flatness for VNA equipment, tolerances under the TR-34 standard and liability for poor slab execution.",
    "excerpt": "In logistics, a few millimetres of flatness decide whether a VNA racking system can operate. The assessment contrasts the executed slab with the contractual tolerance.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-11-17",
    "dateModified": "2026-11-17",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Naves Industriales",
    "tags": [
      "Industrial flooring",
      "TR-34",
      "Flatness",
      "Slab"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Pavimentos industriales: tolerancias TR-34 y disputas de planitud",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Industrial Warehouse Assessment",
        "url": "/naves-industriales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "In a logistics warehouse, the floor is not a finish: it is a work surface with demanding tolerances. A flatness defect or generalised cracking can prevent the operation of high-bay racking (VNA) and generate disputes of considerable value.\n\n## The usual pathologies\n\n**Cracking** from shrinkage or slab settlement, poorly executed or sealed **joints**, surface **spalling** and, above all, **lack of flatness** for the intended equipment. Each has a different cause —ground, mix, curing, execution— that the diagnosis must separate.\n\n## The role of the TR-34 standard\n\nThe technical reference for flatness and regularity tolerances of industrial slabs is the **TR-34** standard (Concrete Society). The assessment contrasts the **flatness actually executed** with the tolerance class **contractually required** by the type of traffic. This contrast objectifies the breach.\n\n## Evidence and repair, two stages\n\nThe report determines the cause, measures the deviation from the tolerance and assesses the extent —the **evidence** stage to claim against the builder under the **LOE**. **Repair** of the slab (regeneration, joint treatment, resin flooring) is a separate stage carried out by a specialist technical contractor.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is cracking in the slab always a defect?**\nNo. Some shrinkage cracking is expected. What matters is its magnitude, its cause and whether it exceeds the allowable.\n\n**What is VNA flatness?**\nThe regularity required for very high-bay racking aisles, far stricter than free traffic.\n\n**Who is the claim against?**\nThe builder, if the defect stems from execution, within the LOE periods."
  },
  {
    "title": "Valuing industrial warehouse damage for the insurer",
    "slug": "industrial-warehouse-damage-valuation-insurer",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/valoracion-danos-nave-industrial-aseguradora/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/industrial-warehouse-damage-valuation-insurer/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/valoracio-danys-nau-industrial-asseguradora/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How industrial warehouse damage is valued for an insurance file: building, stock, steel structure and business interruption, with verifiable expert criteria.",
    "excerpt": "In an industrial loss, valuation decides the indemnity. A verifiable technical report stops the insurer's assessment falling short.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-11-24",
    "dateModified": "2026-11-24",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Naves Industriales",
    "tags": [
      "Damage valuation",
      "Insurance",
      "Industrial warehouse",
      "Loss"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Valoración de daños en nave industrial para la aseguradora",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Industrial Warehouse Assessment",
        "url": "/naves-industriales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When an industrial warehouse suffers a loss —fire, flood, collapse—, the indemnity depends on how the **damage is valued**. A superficial assessment usually undervalues significant items; a verifiable technical report documents them.\n\n## What is valued\n\nA complete file distinguishes several concepts: the **building** (structure, cladding, roof, fixed installations), the **contents** or stock, and, depending on the policy, the **business interruption** (loss of profits) during reinstatement. Each requires a different valuation approach.\n\n## The structure, the critical point\n\nIn fires, the key is to assess whether the **steel structure** retains load-bearing capacity or must be replaced: heat degrades steel before melting it. This decision —repair versus rebuild— is what most affects the amount.\n\n## Why a party report helps\n\nIf the insurer's offer does not cover the proven damage, the policyholder can provide their own report and trigger the **contradictory appraisal (art. 38 LCS)**.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Does the valuation include loss of profits?**\nOnly if the policy covers business interruption. The report documents the time and reinstatement works.\n\n**Is it valued new or with depreciation?**\nIt depends on the policy type (new-for-old reinstatement or actual value).\n\n**Can I dispute the insurer's assessment?**\nYes, through the contradictory appraisal under art. 38 LCS."
  },
  {
    "title": "Public works contract settlement: frequent disputes",
    "slug": "public-works-contract-settlement-disputes",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/liquidacion-contrato-obra-publica-discrepancias/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/public-works-contract-settlement-disputes/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/liquidacio-contracte-obra-publica-discrepancies/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Common disputes in the settlement of a public works contract (measurements, unforeseen items, penalties, price review) and the role of the expert report under the LCSP.",
    "excerpt": "Settlement is where the accumulated differences of the whole works surface. An expert report translates the executed works into the real balance under the LCSP.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-12-01",
    "dateModified": "2026-12-01",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Obra Pública",
    "tags": [
      "LCSP",
      "Settlement",
      "Public works",
      "Measurements"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Liquidación del contrato de obra pública: discrepancias frecuentes",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Public Works Assessment",
        "url": "/obras-publicas/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "The settlement of a public works contract is the moment when the accounts of the whole works are closed, and precisely for that reason it concentrates the accumulated disputes between contractor and public authority. Resolving them requires rigorous technical support under the **LCSP**.\n\n## Where the differences arise\n\nThe most frequent: disputed final **measurements**, **unforeseen work items** executed for the needs of the works, **penalties** for time or quality, **price review** and the treatment of unformalised **variations**.\n\n## What the expert report adds\n\nThe report measures and values what was **actually executed**, contrasts it with the project, the specifications and the certifications, and fits each item into the corresponding legal provision. Against the public authority, the burden of technically justifying the claim falls on the contractor.\n\n## A document to negotiate and to litigate\n\nA sound report facilitates agreement in the settlement itself; failing that, it is the expert evidence in the challenge.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can unexecuted-but-uncertified items be claimed?**\nThe report documents the reality of the executed works, the factual basis of the claim.\n\n**What is the deadline for settlement?**\nThe LCSP sets the deadlines of the settlement procedure; it is advisable to act within them with the technical support ready.\n\n**Does it include price review?**\nYes, where applicable under the contract and the regulations."
  },
  {
    "title": "Prestressed concrete pathologies: assessment in civil works",
    "slug": "prestressed-concrete-pathologies-civil-works",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/patologias-hormigon-pretensado-obra-civil/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/prestressed-concrete-pathologies-civil-works/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/patologies-formigo-pretensat-obra-civil/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Assessment of pathologies in prestressed concrete structures (viaducts, bridges, decks): cracking, tendon corrosion, prestress losses and structural safety evaluation.",
    "excerpt": "Prestressed concrete concentrates the most delicate civil-works pathologies: a tendon failure is not visible, but it compromises the whole structure.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-12-08",
    "dateModified": "2026-12-08",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Obra Pública",
    "tags": [
      "Prestressed concrete",
      "Viaduct",
      "Civil works",
      "Structural pathology"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Patologías en hormigón pretensado: peritaje en obra civil",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Public Works Assessment",
        "url": "/obras-publicas/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "Prestressed concrete structures —viaducts, bridges, decks, large slabs— are among the most efficient in civil engineering and, at the same time, among those requiring the most specialist diagnosis when they show pathologies. A prestress failure is rarely visible, but it affects the whole.\n\n## The characteristic pathologies\n\nAnomalous **cracking** from prestress loss or execution defects, **tendon corrosion** (favoured by grouting defects in the ducts), **prestress losses** and anchorage damage. To these are added the pathologies common to reinforced concrete: carbonation, chlorides, bearing settlement.\n\n## Why it requires specific criteria\n\nThe behaviour of prestressing cannot be analysed with the same criteria as a conventional reinforced structure. Diagnosis combines inspection, testing and **structural modelling** under the Eurocode to assess residual load-bearing capacity and safety.\n\n## Expert context\n\nThese pathologies usually appear in high-value contexts: disputes with the builder, claims against the public authority or liability files. The report distinguishes liability with regulatory references.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can prestressing be assessed without destroying the structure?**\nLargely yes, through inspection techniques and non-destructive testing, complemented by calculation.\n\n**Is a crack in a deck serious?**\nIt depends on its origin and layout. In prestressed concrete, certain cracks reveal prestress loss.\n\n**Who is liable for this damage?**\nDepending on the origin: design, execution or maintenance. The report delimits it."
  },
  {
    "title": "Party-appointed expert against the insurer: preparing the claim",
    "slug": "party-expert-against-insurer-claim",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/perito-de-parte-frente-aseguradora-reclamacion/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/party-expert-against-insurer-claim/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/perit-de-part-davant-asseguradora-reclamacio/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How the policyholder prepares a claim against their company with a party-appointed expert: loss documentation, independent valuation and the article 38 LCS routes.",
    "excerpt": "When the company undervalues or rejects the loss, the policyholder is not defenceless: a party expert rebalances the relationship with the insurer.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-12-15",
    "dateModified": "2026-12-15",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Seguros",
    "tags": [
      "Party-appointed expert",
      "Insurer",
      "Claim",
      "Art. 38 LCS"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Perito de parte frente a la aseguradora: cómo se prepara la reclamación",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Counter-Report",
        "url": "/contrainforme-pericial/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "In a construction loss, the relationship between policyholder and company is asymmetric: the insurer has its own experts and criteria to value the damage. A **party-appointed expert** rebalances that relationship by providing an independent technical valuation.\n\n## When it intervenes\n\nWhen the company **undervalues** the damage, **excludes** items or **rejects** cover alleging an uncovered origin. In all these cases, the dispute is technical before it is legal.\n\n## How the claim is prepared\n\nThe expert documents the loss with verifiable methodology, determines the **origin** of the damage —on which cover depends—, quantifies the extent and contrasts the disputed items. That report is the basis for negotiating and, failing agreement, for triggering the **contradictory appraisal (art. 38 LCS)** or the court route.\n\n## The value of acting early\n\nThe sooner the loss is documented, the better the evidence is preserved. Removing debris or repairing without a report can weaken the claim.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can I claim even if I have already accepted a payment on account?**\nA payment on account does not prevent claiming the difference if the real damage is higher.\n\n**What if the company does not accept my report?**\nThe contradictory appraisal under art. 38 LCS is triggered.\n\n**Is it the same as a counter-report?**\nNo. The counter-report rebuts the opposing expert's report; the party report substantiates your own claim."
  },
  {
    "title": "Flood damage expert: what the report documents",
    "slug": "flood-damage-expert-report",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/perito-danos-inundacion-dictamen/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/flood-damage-expert-report/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/perit-danys-inundacio-dictamen/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What an expert report documents after a flood: origin of the water, extent to structure and installations, deferred damage and valuation for the claim to the insurer or the liable party.",
    "excerpt": "After a flood, much of the damage is not visible on the first day. The report also documents deferred damage, which the quick assessment usually ignores.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-12-22",
    "dateModified": "2026-12-22",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Seguros",
    "tags": [
      "Flood",
      "Water damage",
      "Assessment",
      "Valuation"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Perito de daños por inundación: qué documenta el dictamen",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Moisture and Water Leakage Report",
        "url": "/humedades-filtraciones/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "Floods —from extraordinary rain, overflow or a burst main— leave damage that rarely stops at what is visible on the first day. An expert report documents both the immediate damage and the **deferred** damage, which is where the quick assessment usually falls short.\n\n## What the report documents\n\nThe **origin and mechanics** of the water entry (key for cover), the **extent** in structure, cladding, installations (electrical, HVAC) and finishes, and the **deferred damage**: residual damp, corrosion, loss of insulation, pathologies that appear weeks later.\n\n## Why deferred damage matters\n\nAn assessment made in the heat of the moment can undervalue the loss by not contemplating the evolution of damp in materials and structure. The report anticipates these effects with technical criteria.\n\n## The claim context\n\nDepending on the case, the claim is directed at the **insurer**, a **liable third party** or the public authority. Repair and drying are then carried out by a specialist contractor.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Does home insurance cover a flood?**\nIt depends on the origin and the policy; damage from extraordinary events may correspond to the Insurance Compensation Consortium.\n\n**When should the assessment be done, immediately or later?**\nAs soon as possible to preserve the evidence, with monitoring of the deferred damage.\n\n**Can I claim against a neighbour for a leak?**\nIf the origin is in their installation, yes; the report determines the origin."
  },
  {
    "title": "Challenging the expert's impartiality (LEC 343): when it applies and how it is raised",
    "slug": "challenging-expert-impartiality-lec-343",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/tacha-del-perito-lec-343/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/challenging-expert-impartiality-lec-343/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/tatxa-del-perit-lec-343/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What the challenge of experts under article 343 of the LEC is, how it differs from recusal, when it applies and how it affects the evidential value of the opposing report.",
    "excerpt": "The challenge does not dispute the content of the report, but the expert's impartiality. It is a different —and complementary— tool from the counter-report.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-12-29",
    "dateModified": "2026-12-29",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Contrainforme",
    "tags": [
      "Expert challenge",
      "LEC 343",
      "Impartiality",
      "Expert evidence"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Tacha del perito (LEC 343): cuándo procede y cómo se plantea",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Counter-Report",
        "url": "/contrainforme-pericial/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "In proceedings with expert evidence, questioning the opposing report has two different routes: attacking its **technical content** (the counter-report) or questioning the **expert's impartiality** (the challenge, *tacha*). They should not be confused.\n\n## What the challenge is\n\nThe **challenge of experts** (**LEC art. 343**) is directed against experts **who authored reports submitted by the parties** —not court-appointed ones— when there are circumstances casting doubt on their impartiality: kinship, interest in the matter, friendship or enmity, dependence. It does not annul the report, but **the judge takes it into account**.\n\n## Difference from recusal\n\n**Recusal** is reserved for court-appointed experts and, if successful, removes the expert. The **challenge** affects party experts and operates on **credibility**.\n\n## How it works with the counter-report\n\nThe challenge questions **who** signs; the counter-report questions **what** it says. Used together, they reinforce the objection before the judge's free evaluation (**LEC art. 348**).\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Does the challenge invalidate the report?**\nNo. The judge takes it into account when evaluating the evidence, but the report remains in the proceedings.\n\n**When is it raised?**\nAt the procedural moment provided, usually on learning of the report and the expert.\n\n**Is the challenge enough to win?**\nRarely. What is effective is combining it with a counter-report."
  },
