      }
    ],
    "content": "For a law firm, construction expert evidence is often what decides the case. Its effectiveness is played out in the **preparation**: choosing the expert well, defining the object and anticipating ratification.\n\n## Choice and instruction\n\nThe expert must combine accredited technical competence with the ability to **defend the report** orally. The instruction must precisely define the **object**: the technical questions the report must answer, aligned with the procedural strategy.\n\n## The report as evidence\n\nA good report is **traceable**: each conclusion follows from the data and the standard (**CTE, EHE-08, LOE**). It anticipates the opposing expert's objections and documents the quantification.\n\n## Ratification and cross-examination\n\nOral **ratification** (**LEC art. 347**) is where the report holds or weakens. Coordinating in advance the lines of examination and the opposing party's likely questions is part of the lawyer's work with their expert.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Party report or request a court expert?**\nIt depends on the strategy. The party report gives control and initiative; the court expert brings neutrality.\n\n**Can the expert attend the trial even if they do not ratify that day?**\nRatification is carried out at the hearing; their availability should be planned.\n\n**What distinguishes a winning report?**\nMethodological soundness and a clear, composed ratification."
  },
  {
    "title": "Ten-year warranty and decennial insurance: how it is activated",
    "slug": "ten-year-warranty-decennial-insurance",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/garantia-decenal-seguro-decenal-activar/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/ten-year-warranty-decennial-insurance/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/garantia-desenal-asseguranca-desenal-activar/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What the LOE ten-year warranty covers, how the developer's compulsory decennial insurance works and the role of the expert report in activating cover for structural damage.",
    "excerpt": "The ten-year warranty covers damage compromising the building's stability for ten years. Activating it requires establishing the structural origin of the damage.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-01-12",
    "dateModified": "2027-01-12",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Ten-year warranty",
      "Decennial insurance",
      "LOE",
      "Structural damage"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Garantía decenal y seguro decenal: cómo se activa",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "The **ten-year warranty** is the broadest cover of the **LOE (art. 17)**: it protects for **ten years** against damage that **compromises the stability and resistance** of the building. Activating it requires technically establishing the structural origin of the damage.\n\n## What it covers\n\nOnly damage affecting the **structural elements** —foundation, beams, slabs, load-bearing walls— and compromising safety. It does not cover habitability defects (3-year warranty) or finishing defects (1 year). The period runs from the **handover of the works**.\n\n## The decennial insurance\n\nThe developer is obliged to take out **decennial insurance** backing this warranty. In the event of structural damage, the claim can be directed at the decennial insurer, in addition to the responsible parties under the LOE liability regime.\n\n## The role of the expert report\n\nThe report establishes that the damage is **structural**, that it **compromises stability** and which party is **responsible**, as well as quantifying the repair. Without that support, the claim does not succeed.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Does every crack trigger the ten-year warranty?**\nNo. Only damage affecting structural stability.\n\n**When do the ten years start?**\nFrom the handover certificate of the works.\n\n**Who do I claim against, the developer or the insurer?**\nIt can be directed at both; the report and the lawyer determine the most effective route."
  },
  {
    "title": "Who pays the expert in a lawsuit?",
    "slug": "who-pays-the-expert-in-a-lawsuit",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/quien-paga-el-perito-en-un-juicio/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/who-pays-the-expert-in-a-lawsuit/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/qui-paga-el-perit-en-un-judici/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Who bears the expert's fees in civil proceedings: party-appointed expert, court-appointed expert, advance of funds and the effect of a costs order.",
    "excerpt": "Each party advances its own expert; the final split depends on the type of appointment and the costs order. Worth understanding before litigating.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-01-19",
    "dateModified": "2027-01-19",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Proceso",
    "tags": [
      "Fees",
      "Court-appointed expert",
      "Costs",
      "Advance of funds"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "¿Quién paga el perito en un juicio?",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Fees and cost",
        "url": "/honorarios-perito-judicial-barcelona/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Court-Appointed Expert Witness",
        "url": "/perito-judicial/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "One of the first questions for anyone facing a construction lawsuit is who pays the expert. The answer depends on whether the expert is **party-appointed** or **court-appointed**, and on the outcome of the proceedings.\n\n## The party-appointed expert\n\nPaid by **the party who commissions it**. It is an investment under its control. If the proceedings end with a **costs order** against the other party, the party expert's fees may be included, within the limits of the costs assessment.\n\n## The court-appointed expert\n\nWhen appointed by the court, it sets an **advance of funds** which, as a rule, is advanced by the party that requested the evidence. The final split is adjusted with the decision and, where applicable, the costs order.\n\n## The effect of costs\n\nIn general terms, the party whose claims are rejected may be ordered to pay the costs, which may include the expert fees within the legal limits.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Do I recover what I paid the expert if I win?**\nIt may be recovered wholly or partly through the costs order, within the limits of the assessment.\n\n**What is the advance of funds?**\nThe amount the court sets for the court-appointed expert before the report is issued.\n\n**How much does an expert report cost?**\nIt depends on the complexity of the case; a detailed estimate of the scope is advisable."
  },
  {
    "title": "Ratification of the report in court: what it is and why it weighs",
    "slug": "expert-report-ratification-in-court",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/ratificacion-informe-pericial-sala/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/expert-report-ratification-in-court/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/ratificacio-informe-pericial-sala/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What ratification of an expert report at trial is, how cross-examination proceeds and why the oral defence of the report determines its evidential force.",
    "excerpt": "A flawless report can lose value if it is not defended competently in court. Ratification is where the report holds up before the judge.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-01-26",
    "dateModified": "2027-01-26",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Proceso",
    "tags": [
      "Ratification",
      "Cross-examination",
      "LEC 347",
      "Expert evidence"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "La ratificación del dictamen en sala: qué es y por qué pesa",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Court-Appointed Expert Witness",
        "url": "/perito-judicial/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "An expert report does not end when it is delivered: it is completed at **ratification**, the moment when the expert defends the report orally before the judge and the lawyers. It is a stage many underestimate and which, nonetheless, determines much of its evidential force.\n\n## What ratification is\n\nUnder the **LEC (art. 347)**, at trial the expert ratifies the report and answers questions from the parties and the court. Ratification turns the document into living evidence, subject to challenge.\n\n## Cross-examination\n\nThe lawyers of both parties may examine the expert to reinforce or dismantle their conclusions. A competent expert explains complex technical matters clearly and does not contradict themselves under pressure. The **soundness of that defence** is what the judge weighs.\n\n## Why it weighs so much\n\nThe judge evaluates expert evidence under the **free evaluation standard (LEC art. 348)**. Between two contradictory reports, they will tend to give more credit to the expert who defends the report with rigour and composure.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is ratification compulsory?**\nIf a party requests it, the expert must attend to ratify; otherwise the report may lose effect.\n\n**Is ratification charged separately?**\nIt depends on the professional. Confirming whether it is included in the instruction is advisable.\n\n**Can the judge question the expert directly?**\nYes. The court may ask for clarification to form its view."
  },
  {
    "title": "Hidden defects or new-build defects: LOE or Civil Code?",
    "slug": "hidden-defects-vs-new-build-defects-loe-cc",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/vicios-ocultos-vs-defectos-obra-nueva-loe-cc/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/hidden-defects-vs-new-build-defects-loe-cc/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/vicis-ocults-vs-defectes-obra-nova-loe-cc/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How to tell whether a claim for defects in a home is governed by the LOE (new build) or by the Civil Code (hidden defects in a sale), and why the framing conditions periods and liable parties.",
    "excerpt": "The same defect can be claimed through two different legal routes depending on how the home was acquired. Choosing the wrong route means losing periods and liable parties.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-02-02",
    "dateModified": "2027-02-02",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Vicios Ocultos",
    "tags": [
      "Hidden defects",
      "LOE",
      "Civil Code",
      "Construction defects"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Vicios ocultos o defectos de obra nueva: ¿LOE o Código Civil?",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Hidden Defects Report",
        "url": "/vicios-ocultos/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "The same defect —damp, a crack, a faulty installation— can be claimed through two different legal routes depending on the context of acquisition. Framing it correctly from the outset is decisive, because **periods and liable parties change**.\n\n## The LOE route\n\nDefects in a **new-build home** are governed by the **Building Act**. Its art. 17 organises the liability of the parties with warranty periods of **1, 3 and 10 years** according to seriousness, counted from the handover of the works.\n\n## The Civil Code route\n\n**Hidden defects** in a **sale** (typically between private individuals, or second-hand) are governed by the **Civil Code (arts. 1484 ff.)**, with a **6-month** period from delivery.\n\n## Why the framing matters\n\nThe periods and liable parties are different. Applying the wrong route can lead to claiming out of time. The expert report provides the **technical facts** —origin, seriousness, age—; the legal framing is a matter for the lawyer.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Did I buy off-plan, LOE or Civil Code?**\nBuying a new build from the developer usually falls within the LOE.\n\n**And if I bought second-hand?**\nThe usual route is the Civil Code hidden defects, with the 6-month period.\n\n**Can more than one action coexist?**\nIn certain cases yes. The technical report serves any of them."
  },
  {
    "title": "Thermography in damp assessment: what it detects and what it does not",
    "slug": "thermography-damp-assessment-what-it-detects",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/termografia-peritaje-humedades-que-detecta/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/thermography-damp-assessment-what-it-detects/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/termografia-peritatge-humitats-que-detecta/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How infrared thermography is used in damp assessment, what it can detect, its limits and why it is combined with other techniques to locate the origin of the water.",
    "excerpt": "Thermography is the star tool of damp diagnosis, but it does not see water: it interprets thermal patterns. Knowing how to read them is what makes it useful.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-02-09",
    "dateModified": "2027-02-09",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Humedades",
    "tags": [
      "Thermography",
      "Damp",
      "Diagnosis",
      "CTE DB-HS"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Termografía en el peritaje de humedades: qué detecta y qué no",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Moisture and Water Leakage Report",
        "url": "/humedades-filtraciones/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "**Infrared thermography** has become the reference tool of damp diagnosis, to the point that it is sometimes credited with powers it does not have. It is worth understanding what it really detects —and what it does not.\n\n## What it detects\n\nThe thermal camera does not \"see\" water: it measures **surface temperatures**. A damp zone is usually cooler through evaporation, and materials with different moisture show different thermal patterns. Interpreting those patterns, the technician locates affected areas and **anomalies** that point towards the origin.\n\n## What it does not detect on its own\n\nThermography does not quantify moisture or confirm its nature. That is why it is **combined** with depth hygrometers, watertightness tests and analysis of the construction detail. It also depends on conditions: it requires sufficient thermal contrast and expert reading of the results.\n\n## Its role in the report\n\nUsed correctly, thermography allows a **non-destructive** diagnosis: locating the origin without opening up the works. It is a piece of the method, not a verdict in itself (**CTE DB-HS**).\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is a thermal photo enough to claim?**\nNo. It is one element of the diagnosis; the report integrates it with other evidence.\n\n**Does it work at any time?**\nIt needs thermal contrast. Some conditions are more favourable than others.\n\n**Does it detect damp inside the wall?**\nIt detects its effects on the surface; depth is confirmed with hygrometers."
  },
  {
    "title": "Openings in load-bearing walls: assessing poorly executed works",
    "slug": "openings-in-load-bearing-walls-poor-execution",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/aperturas-muros-carga-intervenciones-mal-ejecutadas/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/openings-in-load-bearing-walls-poor-execution/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/obertures-murs-carrega-intervencions-mal-executades/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Risks of opening gaps in load-bearing walls without a project or adequate reinforcement, how poorly executed works are assessed and what structural damage can result.",
    "excerpt": "Opening a gap in a load-bearing wall without calculation or reinforcement is one of the most frequent causes of structural damage in renovations. The assessment establishes it.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-02-16",
    "dateModified": "2027-02-16",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Patologías Estructurales",
    "tags": [
      "Load-bearing wall",
      "Renovation",
      "Reinforcement",
      "Structural intervention"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Aperturas en muros de carga: peritaje de intervenciones mal ejecutadas",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "Opening a gap in a load-bearing wall is a structural intervention, even though it is often carried out as if it were a minor renovation. When done without a project, calculation or adequate reinforcement, one of the most frequent structural damages in residential building appears.\n\n## Why it is delicate\n\nA load-bearing wall transmits the loads of the upper floors. When a gap is opened, those loads must be diverted through a properly sized and supported **lintel or reinforcement**. If the reinforcement is inadequate, poorly executed or the temporary shoring is missing, the wall and the elements above it suffer: cracks, sagging and, in serious cases, risk of local collapse.\n\n## What the assessment establishes\n\nThe report determines whether the intervention was carried out according to good practice (**lex artis**) and the structural standards, whether the reinforcement is adequate for the real loads, and what damage results from poor execution. It distinguishes liability and values the repair.\n\n## Evidence and solution\n\nThe report proves the defect and its extent; the **corrective reinforcement** is then designed and executed by a specialist contractor from the diagnosis.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Do I need permission to open a gap in a load-bearing wall?**\nYes: it requires a technical project and a licence. Doing it without them aggravates liability.\n\n**How do I know if my wall is load-bearing?**\nIt is not always obvious; its identification is part of the technical diagnosis.\n\n**Can I claim against whoever did the renovation?**\nIf the work was deficient, the report substantiates the poor-workmanship claim."
  },
  {
    "title": "RSCIEI: common breaches and their effect in a claim",
    "slug": "rscei-common-breaches-in-a-claim",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/rscei-incumplimientos-frecuentes-siniestro/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/rscei-common-breaches-in-a-claim/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/rscei-incompliments-frequents-sinistre/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What the RSCIEI is, the most common breaches in industrial warehouses and how they affect insurance cover and the allocation of liability after a fire.",
    "excerpt": "Complying with the industrial fire regulation not only avoids penalties: in a claim, it conditions cover and the allocation of liability.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-02-23",
    "dateModified": "2027-02-23",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Naves Industriales",
    "tags": [
      "RSCIEI",
      "Fire",
      "Industrial warehouse",
      "Regulations"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "RSCIEI: incumplimientos frecuentes y su efecto en un siniestro",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Industrial Warehouse Assessment",
        "url": "/naves-industriales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "The **RSCIEI** —Regulation on Fire Safety in Industrial Establishments (RD 2267/2004)— sets the fire-protection conditions a warehouse must meet. Its relevance is not merely administrative: in a claim, **a prior breach may condition insurance cover and the allocation of liability**.\n\n## Common breaches\n\nInsufficient compartmentation, **fire stability** of the structure below the required level, protection installations (detection, sprinklers, hydrants) missing or unmaintained, inadequate escape routes, or an incorrect **risk characterisation**.\n\n## Effect in a claim\n\nAfter a fire, the assessment checks whether the warehouse complied with the RSCIEI **at the time of the loss**. A significant breach may aggravate the extent of the damage, affect the policy **cover** and affect the **allocation of liability**.\n\n## What the report documents\n\nThe report contrasts the real situation with the regulatory requirements, relates the breaches to the evolution of the loss and provides the technical basis for the claim or the defence.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Does a breach always void cover?**\nNot necessarily; it depends on its significance and its causal link with the damage.\n\n**Who is liable for an RSCIEI breach?**\nDepending on the case: the owner, the installation designer or the maintenance provider.\n\n**Does it only affect fires?**\nThe RSCIEI is specific to industrial fires, but compliance also affects the risk assessment."
  },
  {
    "title": "Expert to claim against the builder: what your case needs",
    "slug": "expert-to-claim-against-builder-what-you-need",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/perito-reclamar-constructora-que-necesita/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/expert-to-claim-against-builder-what-you-need/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/perit-reclamar-constructora-que-necessita/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What documentation and technical analysis a claim against the builder for defects or poor execution needs: contract, specification, evidence of the defect and quantification.",
    "excerpt": "Claiming against the builder without technical support is a word-against-word argument. The report turns the complaint into a defensible position.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-03-02",
    "dateModified": "2027-03-02",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Claim against builder",
      "Poor execution",
      "LOE",
      "Defects"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Perito para reclamar a la constructora: qué necesita su caso",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "Claiming against a builder for defects or breaches, without technical support, usually ends in a clash of perceptions. An expert report turns that complaint into a **defensible position**, in negotiation or in court.\n\n## What documentation helps\n\nThe case is reinforced by the **works contract**, the **budget**, the **specification**, the project and any communication with the builder. Against those documents, the assessment contrasts what was **actually executed**.\n\n## What the report analyses\n\nThe report identifies each **defect or breach**, determines its **cause**, fits it into the corresponding warranty period (**LOE art. 17**: 1, 3 or 10 years) and **quantifies** the cost of remedy.\n\n## The claim process\n\nOften, a professional report is enough for the builder to **remedy** without going to court. If there is no agreement, mediation with a technical basis is possible and, as a last resort, court proceedings, with **ratification** included.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Do I need the contract to claim?**\nIt helps a lot, but it is not essential: good construction practice (lex artis) serves as a reference.\n\n**And if I have already paid for the whole works?**\nHaving paid does not prevent claiming for defects or for items not carried out.\n\n**How long does it take?**\nThe diagnosis depends on the scope; acting within the LOE warranty periods is advisable."
  },
  {
