      }
    ],
    "content": "When the company's appraisal does not reflect the real damage, the policyholder is not obliged to accept it. Insurance law provides an orderly route to disagree, and it is worth following it in the right order.\n\n## First: understand and document\n\nThe starting point is an independent **party report** establishing the origin of the damage and its real valuation. Without that support, the disagreement is an assertion; with it, it is a technical position.\n\n## The contradictory appraisal (art. 38 LCS)\n\nIf disagreement over the **amount** persists, **article 38 of the Insurance Contract Act** provides for the contradictory appraisal: each party appoints an expert and, failing agreement, a **third expert** is appointed. The procedure has strict deadlines.\n\n## Then: the court route\n\nIf the art. 38 procedure does not resolve it, the party report is expert evidence in the court claim. The insurer's expert report can also be questioned through a **counter-report**.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can I refuse to sign the company's report?**\nYes. Signing in agreement may close the claim; when in doubt, seek advice first.\n\n**How much does it cost to disagree?**\nThe cost of your own expert and, where applicable, of the art. 38 procedure.\n\n**Is there a deadline to react?**\nYes, and it is strict in the contradictory appraisal. Act without delay."
  },
  {
    "title": "Difference between the ITE and the IEE building assessments",
    "slug": "difference-between-ite-and-iee",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/diferencia-ite-iee/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/difference-between-ite-and-iee/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/diferencia-ite-iee/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Differences between the Technical Building Inspection (ITE) and the Building Evaluation Report (IEE): what each assesses, when they are mandatory and how they relate.",
    "excerpt": "The ITE and IEE are confused but are not the same: one assesses the state of conservation; the other adds accessibility and energy efficiency.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-03-16",
    "dateModified": "2027-03-16",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "ITE",
    "tags": [
      "ITE",
      "IEE",
      "Building inspection",
      "Regulations"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Diferencia entre ITE e IEE: qué evalúa cada una",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/patologias-estructurales/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Structural Consulting",
        "url": "/asesoramiento-estructural/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "The **ITE** and the **IEE** are often mentioned as synonyms, but they assess different things. Understanding the difference avoids confusion about what is mandatory and what information each document provides.\n\n## The ITE\n\nThe **Technical Building Inspection** assesses the **state of conservation** of the building: structure, facades, roofs, communal installations. Its aim is to detect defects affecting safety and require their remedy. It is mandatory for buildings above a certain age.\n\n## The IEE\n\nThe **Building Evaluation Report** is broader: it incorporates the state of conservation (equivalent to the ITE) and adds the assessment of **accessibility conditions** and **energy efficiency**. It is usually required in certain procedures, such as access to renovation grants.\n\n## How they relate\n\nIn practice, the IEE **encompasses** the ITE in its conservation section and complements it. When the inspection detects structural defects, a **specific study** to determine cause, extent and solution is advisable.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Do I have to do the ITE and IEE separately?**\nIt depends on the procedure and the applicable regulations. In many cases, the IEE also covers the conservation requirement.\n\n**Who can sign them?**\nCompetent technicians (architects, technical architects, engineers as applicable).\n\n**What if serious defects are detected?**\nA specific diagnosis to determine the cause and prioritise the works is advisable."
  },
  {
    "title": "Hidden-defects expert in Barcelona: when to commission the report",
    "slug": "hidden-defects-expert-barcelona-when-to-commission",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/perito-vicios-ocultos-barcelona-cuando-encargar/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/hidden-defects-expert-barcelona-when-to-commission/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/perit-vicis-ocults-barcelona-quan-encarregar/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "When to commission an expert report for hidden defects after buying a home in Barcelona, so as not to lose the Civil Code 6-month time limit.",
    "excerpt": "With hidden defects, time works against you: the 6-month period runs from delivery. Commissioning the report early is what saves the claim.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-03-23",
    "dateModified": "2027-03-23",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Vicios Ocultos",
    "tags": [
      "Hidden defects",
      "Barcelona",
      "Time limit",
      "Sale"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Perito de vicios ocultos en Barcelona: cuándo encargar el informe",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Hidden Defects Report",
        "url": "/vicios-ocultos/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "If you have just bought a home in Barcelona and discovered a serious defect that was not visible, the key question is not only whether you are entitled to claim, but **when** to commission the expert report. With hidden defects, time is decisive.\n\n## The period that conditions everything\n\nThe **Civil Code (art. 1490)** sets **6 months** from delivery of the property to bring hidden-defects actions. It is a **limitation** period: it is not interrupted or extended. The expert report must be obtained **within** that period to establish the existence, seriousness and **pre-existence** of the defect.\n\n## Why commission it early\n\nThe sooner the defect is documented, the better it is proven that it **already existed** before the purchase, and the more room remains to negotiate before the period runs out. Waiting reduces the options.\n\n## What to do on detecting the problem\n\nImmediately document the defect (photographs, dates), request the expert report and coordinate the notification to the seller with the lawyer.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**When do the 6 months start?**\nFrom delivery of the property, not from when the defect is discovered.\n\n**And if I discover the defect in the fifth month?**\nAct urgently: commission the report and claim before expiry.\n\n**Does it apply to second-hand homes?**\nYes, it is the typical case of Civil Code hidden defects."
  },
  {
    "title": "A second expert to contradict a report: when it adds value",
    "slug": "second-expert-to-contradict-a-report-when",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/segundo-perito-contradecir-informe-cuando/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/second-expert-to-contradict-a-report-when/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/segon-perit-contradir-informe-quan/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "When it is worth providing a second expert or counter-report to rebut an opposing report, what it must show and how it influences the judge's evaluation.",
    "excerpt": "Providing a second expert does not always add value: it does when it shows concrete deficiencies in the opposing report, not when it merely offers another opinion.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-03-30",
    "dateModified": "2027-03-30",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Contrainforme",
    "tags": [
      "Second expert",
      "Counter-report",
      "Expert evidence",
      "LEC 348"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Segundo perito para contradecir un informe: cuándo aporta valor",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Counter-Report",
        "url": "/contrainforme-pericial/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When the opposing party provides an unfavourable report, the question arises whether a **second expert** to contradict it is worthwhile. The answer depends on what that second report can show: it adds value when it points out **concrete deficiencies**, not when it merely offers a different opinion.\n\n## Different opinion versus proven deficiency\n\nA judge, faced with two disagreeing experts, evaluates the evidence under the **free evaluation standard (LEC art. 348)**. If the second report merely maintains the opposite, the result is a technical draw. By contrast, a **counter-report** showing that the opposing report is incomplete, incorrect or methodologically deficient **tips the balance**.\n\n## What it must show\n\nThe counter-report analyses the rigour of the inspection, the correctness of the regulatory references, the coherence between data and conclusions and the soundness of the quantification.\n\n## When it is worthwhile\n\nWhen the opposing report has real shortcomings; when the amount at stake justifies the cost; and when there is procedural scope to provide it.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is it enough to say I disagree?**\nNo. You must show why the opposing report is deficient.\n\n**How long does a counter-report take?**\nUsually between 10 and 20 working days.\n\n**Is it combined with challenging the expert?**\nYes; the challenge questions impartiality and the counter-report the content. They are complementary."
  },
  {
    "title": "Advance of funds and fees of the appointed expert",
    "slug": "advance-of-funds-fees-appointed-expert",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/provision-fondos-honorarios-perito-designado/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/advance-of-funds-fees-appointed-expert/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/provisio-fons-honoraris-perit-designat/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How the advance of funds for a court-appointed expert works, who pays it, how fees are set and what happens if a party does not deposit it.",
    "excerpt": "The advance of funds is the payment the court orders before the expert works. Understanding how it works avoids surprises in the proceedings.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-04-06",
    "dateModified": "2027-04-06",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Proceso",
    "tags": [
      "Advance of funds",
      "Fees",
      "Court-appointed expert",
      "LEC"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Provisión de fondos y honorarios del perito designado",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Fees and cost",
        "url": "/honorarios-perito-judicial-barcelona/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Court-Appointed Expert Witness",
        "url": "/perito-judicial/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When the court appoints an expert, before they begin work an **advance of funds** is usually set: an advance on their fees. Knowing how it works avoids surprises during the proceedings.\n\n## What it is and who pays it\n\nThe advance of funds is the amount the expert requests and the court approves as a **guarantee and advance** of their fees. As a rule, it is deposited by the **party that proposed the evidence**; if it interests both, it can be shared.\n\n## How fees are set\n\nFees depend on the **complexity** of the report: scope, tests, travel, analysis time and ratification. They are not set by the figure (party or court), but by the work.\n\n## If a party does not deposit it\n\nIf the party required to deposit the advance does not, the expert evidence may **not be carried out**, with the procedural consequences that entails.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is the advance the final cost?**\nIt is an advance; the final amount is adjusted to the work carried out.\n\n**Is it recovered with costs?**\nIt may be recovered wholly or partly through the costs order.\n\n**Can I ask for an estimate beforehand?**\nFor the party report, yes: a detailed estimate of the scope is advisable."
  },
  {
    "title": "Billed but unexecuted works: how they are proven",
    "slug": "billed-but-unexecuted-works-how-to-prove",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/partidas-cobradas-no-ejecutadas-prueba/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/billed-but-unexecuted-works-how-to-prove/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/partides-cobrades-no-executades-prova/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How it is technically established that a builder billed for works not carried out or executed with inferior materials, contrasting budget, specification and actual works.",
    "excerpt": "Charging for what was not done is one of the most frequent breaches and, at the same time, one of the most provable: simply contrast what was billed with what was executed.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-04-13",
    "dateModified": "2027-04-13",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Unexecuted works",
      "Budget",
      "Poor execution",
      "Evidence"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Partidas cobradas y no ejecutadas: cómo se prueban",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Poor Workmanship Report",
        "url": "/reclamacion-mala-ejecucion/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "One of the most frequent breaches in works and renovations is charging for items that were not executed, or executing them with materials inferior to those budgeted. It is also one of the most **provable**, because it is proven by documentary and technical contrast.\n\n## What it is contrasted against\n\nThe assessment compares three references with the **works actually executed**: the accepted **budget**, the **specification** and the **certifications or invoices** issued. From this contrast emerge the billed items not executed, those partially executed and those carried out to inferior quality.\n\n## How it is quantified\n\nThe report measures what was executed, identifies the **deviations** from what was billed and economically values the difference: the amount of unexecuted items, the overprice for inferior materials, and the cost of completing or correcting the works.\n\n## The evidential value\n\nA documented contrast, with measurements and photographs, is hard to rebut. It is usually enough to force an adjustment in negotiation.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**And if I do not have a detailed budget?**\nIt becomes harder, but the specification and good construction practice serve as a reference.\n\n**Can what has already been paid be claimed?**\nYes: what was charged for unexecuted items is an amount unduly received.\n\n**How is an inferior material proven?**\nBy inspection and, if necessary, tests identifying the quality actually used."
  },
  {
    "title": "Claiming damages from the public administration in civil works",
    "slug": "claim-damages-public-administration-civil-works",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/reclamacion-danos-administracion-obra-civil/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/claim-damages-public-administration-civil-works/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/reclamacio-danys-administracio-obra-civil/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "How to substantiate a claim for damages against the public administration in civil works and infrastructure: financial liability, causal link and the role of the expert report.",
    "excerpt": "Claiming against the administration for public-works damage requires proving the causal link between the works and the damage. That is where the report is decisive.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2027-04-20",
    "dateModified": "2027-04-20",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Obra Pública",
    "tags": [
      "Financial liability",
      "Public administration",
      "Civil works",
      "Causal link"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Reclamar daños a la Administración en obra civil",
      "caption": ""
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Public Works Assessment",
        "url": "/obras-publicas/"
      },
      {
        "title": "Party-Appointed Expert Report",
        "url": "/informe-de-parte/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "When public or infrastructure works cause damage to third parties —to neighbouring buildings, installations or an activity—, it is possible to claim against the administration for **financial liability**. The success of that claim depends largely on the technical proof of the **causal link**.\n\n## What must be proven\n\nFinancial liability essentially requires an **actual**, assessable and individualised **damage**, and a **causal relationship** between the operation of the public service (here, the works) and that damage. The critical point is usually the causal link.\n\n## The role of the expert report\n\nThe report documents the damage, analyses its **mechanism** and links it technically to the works, ruling out alternative causes. For example, it relates a building's settlement or cracks to a neighbouring excavation through analysis of the damage pattern and the timeline.\n\n## Procedural context\n\nThe claim is processed administratively and, failing that, before the administrative courts, with its own deadlines. Expert support is transversal to the whole procedure.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is any damage near works compensable?**\nNo. You must prove the works are the cause; coincidence in time is not enough.\n\n**What deadline do I have?**\nThe financial-liability claim has specific deadlines; act without delay.\n\n**Does it apply to damage to my home from municipal works?**\nYes, if the causal link between the works and the damage is established."
  },
  {
    "title": "Hidden Defects in Purchase: Rights and Claim Deadlines",
    "slug": "hidden-defects-purchase-rights-deadlines",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/vicios-ocultos-compraventa-derechos-plazos/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/hidden-defects-purchase-rights-deadlines/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/vicis-ocults-compravenda-drets-terminis/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "fr",
        "permalink": "/fr/blog/vices-caches-achat-droits-delais/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "it",
        "permalink": "/it/blog/vizi-occulti-acquisto-diritti-termini/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Legal and technical information on how to claim for hidden defects after buying a property.",
    "excerpt": "You bought a home and discovered serious problems the seller hid. Learn your legal rights and deadlines to claim successfully.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2025-08-10",
    "dateModified": "2025-08-10",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Hidden Defects",
      "Purchase",
      "Claim",
      "Rights"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Purchase document with magnifying glass",
      "caption": "Hidden defects must be claimed within the established legal deadlines."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Hidden Defects Report",
        "url": "/en/expert-reports/hidden-defects/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## What are Hidden Defects?\n\nAccording to the Civil Code, a hidden defect is a **serious defect that was not apparent** at the time of the sale and that **renders the item unfit for its use** or diminishes its value such that, had the buyer known of it, they would not have acquired it or would have paid a lower price.\n\n## Legal Requirements\n\nFor a defect to be considered a hidden defect it must be:\n\n1. **Existing at the time of sale** (even if it manifests later)\n2. **Not apparent** during an ordinary inspection\n3. **Serious**, compromising the normal use of the property\n4. **Unknown to the buyer**\n\n## Common Examples of Hidden Defects\n\n* Serious structural problems (cracks hidden by cladding)\n* Persistent dampness due to capillarity or leaks\n* Foundation defects (differential settlements)\n* Hidden defective installations (plumbing, electricity)\n* Concrete cancer (aluminosis) or reinforcement corrosion\n\n## Claim Deadlines\n\n### Redhibitory Action (Contract Resolution)\n\n* **6 months** from delivery to report the defect\n* You can demand the **return of the price** and resolution of the sale\n\n### Estimatory Action (Price Reduction)\n\n* **1 year** from delivery\n* A **proportional price reduction** is demanded\n\n**IMPORTANT:** These deadlines are for expiry, not prescription, so they cannot be extended.\n\n## The Role of the Expert Report\n\nA technical expert report is **essential** to:\n\n1. Prove that the defect existed at the time of purchase\n2. Prove that it was not apparent during an ordinary inspection\n3. Assess the severity of the defect and its impact on the property value\n4. Quantify the cost of repair\n5. Provide solid technical evidence in court\n\n## Claim Strategy\n\n1. **Document immediately** the defect (photos, videos)\n2. **Request an expert report** as soon as possible\n3. **Notify the seller** via certified fax (burofax) of the discovered defect\n4. **Negotiate an amicable solution** (repair or price reduction)\n5. If there is no agreement, **file a lawsuit** before the deadline expires\n\n## Conclusion\n\nTime is crucial in claims for hidden defects. An early expert report can make the difference between recovering your money or keeping a defective property.\n\n**Have you discovered a serious problem after buying your home?** Contact us immediately to assess your case."
  },
  {
    "title": "Structural Reinforcements in Rehabilitation: When is shoring necessary?",
    "slug": "structural-reinforcement-building-rehabilitation",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/refuerzo-estructural-rehabilitacion-edificios/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/structural-reinforcement-building-rehabilitation/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/reforc-estructural-rehabilitacio-edificis/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "fr",
        "permalink": "/fr/blog/renforcement-structurel-rehabilitation/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "it",
        "permalink": "/it/blog/rinforzo-strutturale-riabilitazione/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Key information for comprehensive renovations: how to identify load-bearing walls and when a reinforcement project is mandatory.",
    "excerpt": "The desire for 'open spaces' often clashes with structural reality. Learn the risks of knocking down partitions without technical supervision.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2025-09-10",
    "dateModified": "2025-09-10",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Rehabilitation",
    "tags": [
      "Renovations",
      "Structures",
      "Load Bearing Wall",
      "Safety"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/asesoramiento-refuerzo-estructural-barcelona.jpg",
      "alt": "Installation of metal reinforcement beam in rehabilitation work",
      "caption": "Replacement of a load-bearing wall with a metal frame to join spaces."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Advisory",
        "url": "/en/structural-advisory/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## The Danger of 'Open Concept'\n\nThe current trend of joining kitchen and living room implies knocking down partitions. The problem arises when no distinction is made between a simple dividing partition and a **load-bearing wall**. Removing a structural support without adequate reinforcement can cause cracks in the floor of the upper neighbor or, in the worst case, a partial collapse.\n\n## Common Pathologies in Old Buildings\n\nWhen rehabilitating old buildings in Barcelona (Eixample, Ciutat Vella), we often find surprises:\n* **Wood beams with woodworm:** They have lost section and resistance.\n* **Ceramic joists with aluminosis:** They have lost load-bearing capacity.\n* **Deformed walls:** Walls that have buckled over the years.\n\n## The Technical Solution: Shoring and Reinforcements\n\nAny intervention in the structure requires:\n1.  **Load Calculation:** An engineer must calculate how much weight the removed wall supported.\n2.  **Reinforcement Project:** Design the beam (usually HEB or IPE steel profiles) that will replace the wall.\n3.  **Shoring (Propping):** Temporarily support the structure while the work is executed. This is the most critical phase.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nNever knock down a wall 'to see what happens'. A prior structural study is much cheaper than repairing the damage caused by reckless demolition."
  },
