      }
    ],
    "content": "## What is Rising Damp?\n\nRising damp is the phenomenon by which groundwater rises through the pores of building materials, impregnating walls and partitions from their base.\n\n## How to Recognize It\n\nTypical signs include:\n\n* Damp patches on the lower part of walls (up to 1-1.5m height)\n* Peeling paint and plaster\n* Saline efflorescence (crystallized white spots)\n* Persistent smell of damp\n* Deterioration of skirting boards and low joinery\n\n## Main Causes\n\n1. **Absence of damp-proof course** in the foundation (common in old buildings)\n2. **High water table** in the ground\n3. **Breaks in the sanitation network** that dampen the perimeter ground\n4. **Very porous materials** (old ceramic bricks, lime mortars)\n\n## Expert Diagnosis\n\nAn expert must:\n\n* Take measurements with a hygrometer to quantify the level of humidity\n* Rule out other causes (leaks, condensation)\n* Identify the source of the water (water table, leaks, rain)\n* Propose the most appropriate technical solution\n\n## Effective Solutions\n\n### Waterproofing Resin Injection\n\nHoles are drilled at the base of the wall and a resin is injected creating a chemical waterproof barrier.\n\n### Electro-osmosis\n\nAn electrical system is installed that reverses the capillary flow, pushing the water back to the ground.\n\n### Perimeter Drainage\n\nExcavation is done around the building and a drainage system is installed to reduce the water table.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nRising damp requires a definitive solution, not patches. An expert report is essential to choose the correct treatment and avoid wasting money on ineffective solutions."
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Identify Structural Cracks in Your Home",
    "slug": "identify-structural-cracks-home",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/identificar-grietas-estructurales-vivienda/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/identify-structural-cracks-home/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/identificar-esquerdes-estructurals-habitatge/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "fr",
        "permalink": "/fr/blog/identifier-fissures-structurelles-logement/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "it",
        "permalink": "/it/blog/identificare-crepe-strutturali-casa/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Practical guide to distinguish between superficial cracks and structural cracks that compromise the safety of your property.",
    "excerpt": "Not all cracks are the same. Learn to recognize alarm signs indicating a serious structural problem and when it is essential to request an expert report.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2025-10-15",
    "dateModified": "2025-10-15",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Structural Pathologies",
    "tags": [
      "Cracks",
      "Structures",
      "Diagnosis",
      "Safety"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/placeholder-default.jpg",
      "alt": "Structural crack in load-bearing wall",
      "caption": "Diagonal cracks in load-bearing walls can indicate serious foundation problems."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathology Report",
        "url": "/en/expert-reports/structural-pathologies/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## Introduction\n\nThe appearance of cracks in a home is one of the problems that generates the most concern among owners, and rightly so. However, it is fundamental to understand that **not all cracks are the same** nor do they all represent a structural danger.\n\n## Types of Cracks: Fissures vs. Structural Cracks\n\n### Superficial Fissures\n\nFissures are breaks in the coating (paint, plaster) with an opening of less than 1mm. They are generally caused by:\n\n* Temperature and humidity changes\n* Normal building movements\n* Defects in the application of the finish\n* Material shrinkage during drying\n\nThese fissures, although unsightly, **do not compromise the structural safety** of the property.\n\n### Structural Cracks\n\nStructural cracks are openings of more than 1mm that go through the entire thickness of the constructive element (wall, slab, beam). They are much more worrying because they can indicate:\n\n* Foundation problems (differential settlements)\n* Overloads not foreseen in the original calculation\n* Serious defects in the execution of the structure\n* Degradation of materials due to corrosion or deterioration\n\n## Warning Signs: When to Worry\n\nYou should request an urgent expert report if you observe:\n\n1. **Diagonal cracks** in the corners of walls or in the form of stairs following the brick joints\n2. **Horizontal cracks** at the base of walls (may indicate ground thrust)\n3. **Cracks that increase in size** over time (active cracks)\n4. **Cracks that go through the entire thickness** of the wall (visible on both faces)\n5. **Cracks accompanied by other symptoms** such as doors that do not close, uneven floors or detachments\n\n## The Importance of a Professional Diagnosis\n\nA structural expert can:\n\n* Determine if the crack is superficial or structural\n* Identify the root cause of the problem (foundation, overloads, construction defects)\n* Evaluate if there is a risk to the safety of the occupants\n* Propose appropriate technical solutions (reinforcements, micropiles, injections)\n* Quantify the cost of repair for a claim\n\n## Conclusion\n\nWhen in doubt, it is always better to be safe than sorry. An early expert report can prevent a minor problem from turning into a serious structural danger or a costly repair.\n\n**Do you have cracks in your home and don't know if they are worrying?** Contact us immediately to assess your case."
  },
  {
    "title": "Cracked Industrial Pavements: Ground Failure, Bad Execution or Overload?",
    "slug": "industrial-pavement-pathologies-cracks",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/patologias-pavimentos-naves-industriales/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/industrial-pavement-pathologies-cracks/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/patologies-paviments-naus-industrials/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "fr",
        "permalink": "/fr/blog/pathologies-sols-industriels-fissures/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "it",
        "permalink": "/it/blog/patologie-pavimenti-industriali-crepe/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Analysis of the most frequent causes of cracks in industrial floors and solutions to avoid activity stoppages.",
    "excerpt": "A broken floor paralyzes logistics. Discover if your cracks are due to a design failure, bad execution, or misuse, and who should pay for the repair.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2025-10-25",
    "dateModified": "2025-10-25",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Industrial Warehouses",
    "tags": [
      "Pavements",
      "Industrial Warehouses",
      "Cracks",
      "Logistics"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/peritaje-construccion-ingenieros-obra.jpg",
      "alt": "Severe crack in industrial concrete pavement",
      "caption": "Cracks in industrial pavements affect forklift safety and operability."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Reports for Industrial Warehouses",
        "url": "/en/expert-reports/industrial-warehouses/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## The Pavement: The Heart of the Industrial Warehouse\n\nIn a logistics or production warehouse, the floor is not just a surface; it is a work tool. Cracks, unevenness, or subsidence are not only unsightly, but they **damage forklifts, reduce operation speed, and can cause workplace accidents**.\n\n## Common Causes of Slab Pathologies\n\n### 1. Ground Failure (Settlements)\nIf the base on which the concrete was poured was not well compacted, the ground settles over time, leaving voids under the slab. When a load passes, the concrete breaks because it has no support.\n\n### 2. Shrinkage and Lack of Joints\nConcrete shrinks as it dries. If expansion and shrinkage joints were not designed or executed correctly, the material will find its own way to break, creating uncontrolled cracks.\n\n### 3. Usage Overload\nIt is common to see warehouses designed for a specific load that are then used for heavier shelving or machinery for which the floor was not calculated.\n\n## The Expert Diagnosis: Key to Responsibility\n\nDetermining the exact cause is vital, especially in rented or newly built warehouses:\n\n* **If it is bad execution:** The responsibility may lie with the construction company (hidden defects).\n* **If it is misuse:** The responsibility lies with the tenant (post-rental damages).\n* **If it is a design failure:** The responsibility could lie with the engineering or architecture firm.\n\n## Technical Solutions\n\nDepending on the diagnosis, solutions vary from:\n* **Expansive resin injections** to reconsolidate the ground without stopping activity.\n* **Crack stitching** with metal staples and epoxy resin.\n* **Demolition and reconstruction** of the affected slabs (in severe cases).\n\n## Conclusion\n\nDo not ignore cracks in your warehouse. An early diagnosis saves money and avoids costly operational stops."
  },
  {
    "title": "Parking Pillars with Exposed Iron: Diagnosis of Carbonation",
    "slug": "repair-reinforcement-parking-pillars-barcelona",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/reparacion-refuerzo-pilares-parking-barcelona/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/repair-reinforcement-parking-pillars-barcelona/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/reparacio-reforc-pilars-parking-barcelona/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "fr",
        "permalink": "/fr/blog/reparation-renfort-piliers-parking-barcelone/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "it",
        "permalink": "/it/blog/riparazione-rinforzo-pilastri-parcheggio-barcellona/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Technical explanation of why pillars in garages deteriorate and how carbonation puts the structure at risk.",
    "excerpt": "Have you seen fallen concrete and rusty irons on the columns of your parking? It is a serious symptom of carbonation that requires urgent intervention.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2025-11-10",
    "dateModified": "2025-11-10",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Structural Pathologies",
    "tags": [
      "Structures",
      "Carbonation",
      "Parking",
      "Communities"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/patologia-estructural-puente-barcelona.jpg",
      "alt": "Concrete pillar with exposed rusted reinforcement",
      "caption": "Concrete spalling reveals advanced corrosion of the interior reinforcement."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Advisory",
        "url": "/en/structural-advisory/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## The Alarm: Concrete that 'Explodes'\n\nIt is a common image in many garages in Barcelona built decades ago: pieces of concrete on the floor and, on the pillar, steel bars exposed and rusted. Many neighbors mistakenly call it 'aluminosis', but in most cases it is **carbonation**.\n\n## What is Carbonation?\n\nHealthy concrete has a high (alkaline) pH that protects the steel from oxidation. Over time and with CO2 from cars, concrete loses that pH (it carbonates). When moisture reaches the unprotected steel, it oxidizes.\n\n**The problem:** Rust occupies more volume than healthy steel, exerting brutal internal pressure that breaks the concrete from within.\n\n## Is it Dangerous?\n\n**Yes.** If left untreated, corrosion reduces the useful section of steel in the pillar. Less steel means less capacity to support the weight of the building. In advanced stages, it can compromise global stability.\n\n## Solutions: Repair vs. Reinforcement\n\nAn expert report will determine the severity:\n\n1.  **Cosmetic Repair (Slight Damage):** Cleaning rust, passivating steel, and rebuilding with structural mortar.\n2.  **Structural Reinforcement (Severe Damage):** If the pillar has lost a lot of capacity, it needs help. Usually applied:\n    * **Metal jacketing:** Surrounding the pillar with a steel cage.\n    * **Carbon Fiber:** High-strength sheets adhered to the pillar.\n\n## Recommendation for Communities\n\nDo not try to cover cracks with normal cement. That only hides the problem while corrosion advances. Request a technical diagnosis to calculate the necessary reinforcement."
  },
  {
    "title": "I just bought an apartment and it has serious defects: What is considered a 'Hidden Defect'?",
    "slug": "claim-hidden-defects-home-purchase",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/reclama-vicios-ocultos-compraventa-vivienda/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/claim-hidden-defects-home-purchase/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/reclamacio-vicis-ocults-compra-habitatge/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "fr",
        "permalink": "/fr/blog/reclamation-vices-caches-achat-maison/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "it",
        "permalink": "/it/blog/reclamo-vizi-occulti-acquisto-casa/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Legal and technical guide for buyers: deadlines, requirements, and process to claim non-visible defects after purchase.",
    "excerpt": "If damp, termites, or cracks appear after purchase that you didn't see, you may have the right to claim. Learn the difference between hidden defect and wear.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2025-11-25",
    "dateModified": "2025-11-25",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Hidden Defects",
      "Purchase",
      "Claim",
      "Judicial Expert"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/informe-pericial-vicios-ocultos-vivienda-barcelona.jpg",
      "alt": "Hidden mold behind wallpaper in a newly bought home",
      "caption": "Many sellers hide defects aesthetically that reappear after the sale."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Judicial Expert Report",
        "url": "/en/expert-reports/judicial/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## The Golden Deadline: 6 Months\n\nMany buyers are unaware that the Civil Code establishes a very strict deadline: you have **6 months** from the handover of keys to claim for hidden defects. After this time, the action expires and you lose the right.\n\n## The 3 Requirements of the Hidden Defect\n\nNot every defect is claimable. For a judge to consider it a 'hidden defect', it must meet three conditions:\n\n1.  **Hidden:** It must not have been visible during the visit. If it was evident (a huge crack), it is assumed you bought knowing it. *Note: If you are an expert (architect, builder), the criteria is stricter.*\n2.  **Serious:** It must make the home unfit for use or decrease its value so much that, had you known, you would not have bought it or would have paid much less.\n3.  **Pre-existing:** The defect must have existed **before** the purchase, even if it manifested later. This is where the expert report is vital to prove the age of the damage.\n\n## Possible Legal Actions\n\nIf the requirements are confirmed, you can choose:\n* **Redhibitory Action:** Undo the purchase (return the apartment and recover the money + expenses).\n* **Quanti Minoris Action:** Keep the property but demand a price reduction proportional to the repair cost.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nIf you discover a serious defect, do not wait. Document everything, do not repair anything yet, and call a judicial expert immediately to certify pre-existence before the deadline runs out."
  },
  {
    "title": "Slope Stability and Retaining Walls: Expert Assessment, Site Visit and Engineering Solutions",
    "slug": "slope-stability-retaining-walls",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/estabilidad-taludes-muros-contencion/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/estabilitat-talussos-murs-contencio/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/slope-stability-retaining-walls/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Is your retaining wall cracking, has your neighbour excavated at the base, or is your pool leaking near a slope? Slope stability analysis in Barcelona.",
    "excerpt": "A neighbour excavated at the toe of a 6-metre wall while a pool was leaking at the crest. Real case, diagnosis and stabilisation solutions.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-06-26",
    "dateModified": "2026-06-26",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell Serra",
    "category": "Geotechnics",
    "tags": [
      "Slope Stability",
      "Retaining Wall",
      "Geotechnics",
      "Expert Assessment"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/blog/estabilidad-taludes.png",
      "alt": "Composite view of an unstable slope with pool at the crest and excavation at the toe",
      "caption": "Left: crest view. Right: toe with excavation and moisture."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Slope stability assessment",
        "url": "/en/contact/particular/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## When does a slope stop being ground and become a problem?\n\nA slope is any terrain surface inclined with respect to the horizontal. We see them every day and rarely give them a second thought. **Until something changes.**\n\nA neighbour digs at the base. A swimming pool leaks for months. A decades-old party wall starts to lean.\n\nWhen a slope's equilibrium breaks, **the consequences can be catastrophic**.\n\n---\n\n## A real case: pool at the crest, excavation at the toe\n\n**The situation:**\n- A house with a **party wall** bridging a **6-metre** drop.\n- At the top, a **swimming pool** that has been **leaking** for months.\n- The lower neighbour has **excavated a vegetable garden at the toe**.\n\n**The diagnosis:** Three simultaneous destabilising factors:\n\n1. **Surcharge at the crest** — 40+ tonnes of pool.\n2. **Soil saturation** — Leaks reduce cohesion.\n3. **Toe removal** — Excavation eliminates the counterweight.\n\n---\n\n## Stability analysis: what we do\n\n### Phase 1 — Site visit\n\n- Slope geometry, wall condition, drainage, signs of movement.\n- Photographic documentation with georeferencing.\n\n### Phase 2 — Study and analysis\n\n1. Geotechnical characterisation (trial pits, SPT).\n2. Stability model (Bishop, Janbu, Morgenstern-Price).\n3. Earth pressure analysis (Rankine, Coulomb).\n4. Structural verification of the wall.\n\n### Phase 3 — Expert report\n\n- Root cause diagnosis with evidence.\n- Stability calculations with plans.\n- Proposed action with detailed engineering.\n- Liability assessment.\n\n---\n\n## Stabilisation solutions\n\n### Drainage\n- Horizontal drains, drainage trenches, weep holes, crest ditches.\n\n### Retention and reinforcement\n- Riprap or gabion walls, reinforced concrete, reinforced earth, rock bolts, micropiles.\n\n### Re-profiling\n- Slope flattening, equilibrium berm, toe fill.\n\n---\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How much does it cost?\nSite visit + report: **800-1,200 euros**. With geotechnical study: **2,000-4,000 euros**.\n\n### Can I claim against my neighbour?\nYes. Article 1902 of the Spanish Civil Code establishes the obligation to repair the damage.\n\n### Can a swimming pool destabilise a slope?\nAbsolutely. 48 tonnes of concentrated weight plus leaks that saturate the soil."
  },
  {
    "title": "Aluminosis in Barcelona: how to detect it and what to do",
    "slug": "aluminosis-concrete-barcelona-assessment",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/aluminosis-barcelona-deteccion-peritaje/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/aluminosi-barcelona-deteccio-peritatge/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/aluminosis-concrete-barcelona-assessment/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What aluminosis is, how it is detected with the oxine test, how it differs from carbonation, and what to do if your building has it.",
    "excerpt": "The high-alumina cement in many 1950s-70s buildings can lose strength. Learn to spot the symptoms and confirm the diagnosis.",
    "estado": "publicado",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-01",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-01",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell Serra",
    "category": "Structural Pathologies",
    "tags": [
      "Aluminosis",
      "Structural Pathologies",
      "Floor slabs",
      "Barcelona"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/patologia-estructural-puente-barcelona.jpg",
      "alt": "Floor joist with deteriorated concrete and exposed reinforcement",
      "caption": "Diagnosing aluminosis requires a chemical test; visual inspection is not enough."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Structural Pathologies",
        "url": "/en/structural-pathologies/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## What aluminous cement decay (aluminosis) is and why it matters in Barcelona\n\nAluminosis is a pathology of concrete made with **high-alumina cement**, widely used in Spain between the 1950s and 1970s, especially in **floor-slab joists** of residential buildings. Over time, in the presence of heat and moisture, high-alumina cement undergoes a process called **conversion**: its internal structure changes, porosity increases and it **loses strength**. The result is more brittle concrete and reinforcement that is more exposed to corrosion.\n\nBarcelona and its metropolitan area have a large stock of buildings from that period, so aluminosis is still a common enquiry, particularly after cracks or spalling appear in ceilings.\n\n## How it is detected\n\nAluminosis **cannot be diagnosed by eye**. Warning signs include:\n\n* Longitudinal cracks on the underside of the joists.\n* Rust stains or brown discolouration on the ceiling.\n* Spalling of the concrete cover.\n* Visible deflection or deformation of the slab.\n\nConfirmation requires a **chemical test**: a concrete sample is taken and treated with the **oxine** reagent (8-hydroxyquinoline), which stains high-alumina cement yellow-green under ultraviolet light. From there, an expert assessment measures the **degree of conversion** and loss of section to evaluate the real load-bearing capacity.\n\n## Aluminosis is not the same as carbonation\n\nA common confusion. **Carbonation** attacks the steel reinforcement through loss of concrete alkalinity; **aluminosis** attacks the concrete itself because of the cement type. A building may have one, the other or both. Telling them apart is essential, because the remedy and the liability differ.\n\n## What to do if you suspect aluminosis in your building\n\n1. **Do not chip or load** the affected area until it has been diagnosed.\n2. Request an **expert report** that confirms (or rules out) aluminosis and quantifies the structural risk.\n3. With the report, the owners' association can decide the right **reinforcement**: joist replacement, steel reinforcement or carbon-fibre strengthening.\n\nA rigorous diagnosis prevents both unwarranted alarm and dangerous inaction. If you see cracks or spalling in an older building, our [structural pathology report](/en/structural-pathologies/) establishes the real extent and the measures required.\n\n**Unsure about your building's floor slab?** [Tell us about it](/en/contact/particular/) and we will advise you with no obligation."
  },
  {
    "title": "How much does an expert report cost? Prices and factors in 2026",
    "slug": "how-much-does-expert-report-cost",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/cuanto-cuesta-informe-pericial-precios/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/quant-costa-informe-pericial-preus/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/how-much-does-expert-report-cost/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Indicative price ranges for an expert report in Barcelona and the factors that make the fee vary. Fixed written quote.",
    "excerpt": "How much does an assessment cost? Market ranges, what raises the fee, and why to distrust a fixed price over the phone.",
    "estado": "programado",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-08",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-08",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell Serra",
    "category": "Fees",
    "tags": [
      "Fees",
      "Prices",
      "Expert Report",
      "Quote"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/informe-pericial-patologias-barcelona.jpg",
      "alt": "Expert reviewing the technical documentation of a report",
      "caption": "The quote for a report is fixed in writing once the scope of the case is known."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Fees and cost",
        "url": "/en/fees/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## How much an expert report costs: indicative ranges\n\nIt is almost everyone's first question, and the honest answer is: **it depends on the scope**. That said, there are indicative market ranges in Barcelona that help set expectations before asking for a quote:\n\n* **Simple expert report** (one specific pathology, one room, no testing): from around **€300–500**.\n* **Damp or hidden-defect report** with a site visit and instrumental diagnosis: roughly **€500–900**.\n* **Structural pathology report** with calculation, testing or modelling: **from €900**, and it can exceed several thousand depending on complexity.\n* **Court ratification** (attendance and oral defence): sometimes included, sometimes a separate fee.\n\nThese figures are indicative; the real quote is fixed **in writing once the case is known**.\n\n## What makes the price vary\n\n1. **Technical complexity.** Diagnosing a damp patch is not the same as assessing the stability of a structure.\n2. **Testing.** Thermography, test pits, laboratory tests or structural modelling raise the cost but provide objective evidence.\n3. **Area and travel.** The size of the property and the distance from the office matter.\n4. **Urgency.** A very short deadline may carry a surcharge.\n5. **Court use.** A report intended for trial demands greater traceability and, where applicable, oral ratification under the Spanish [Civil Procedure Act (LEC)](/en/court-appointed-expert/).\n\n## Why to distrust a \"fixed price over the phone\"\n\nA fee given without seeing the case usually means one of two things: either the report is superficial, or extra costs will appear later. A serious assessment starts with an **initial consultation** to define the scope and only then issues a **fixed, written quote** with no hidden variable costs. You can see the detail on our [fees](/en/fees/) page.\n\n## Cost versus what is at stake\n\nIt helps to put the fee in context: a report of a few hundred euros can be the evidence that supports a claim worth **tens of thousands**. In hidden defects, damp or structural damage, the report is not an expense but the **technical evidence** that underpins the right to claim.\n\n**Would you like a quote for your case?** [Tell us what is happening](/en/contact/professional/) and we will send you a fixed proposal."
  },
