    "title": "Court-appointed experts: how appointment works and what the report is worth (LEC)",
    "slug": "court-appointed-expert-report-value",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/designacion-perito-judicial-valor-dictamen/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/designacio-perit-judicial-valor-dictamen/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/court-appointed-expert-report-value/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "The difference between party- and court-appointed experts, how list appointment works, and how the judge weighs expert evidence under the LEC.",
    "excerpt": "Party or court expert, appointment by lot and weighing under sound judgement: what every lawyer and affected party should know.",
    "estado": "programado",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-22",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-22",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell Serra",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Court Expert",
      "LEC",
      "Appointment",
      "Expert Evidence"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/peritaje-construccion-ingenieros-obra.jpg",
      "alt": "Expert engineer preparing the documentation of a court report",
      "caption": "The report's weight depends on its traceability and its defence in court."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Court-Appointed Expert",
        "url": "/en/court-appointed-expert/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## Party-appointed and court-appointed experts are not the same\n\nWhen a technical matter reaches the courts, the figure of the expert appears. But it is worth distinguishing two routes, because their procedural weight and how they are appointed differ.\n\n* **Party-appointed expert:** hired directly by one of the parties to produce a report filed with the claim or defence. It is the most common route and the one that allows the case to be prepared with time.\n* **Court-appointed expert:** named by the court from an **official list**, usually by drawing lots, at a party's request or on the court's own motion. The report is perceived as especially impartial because it comes from the court's own appointment.\n\nBoth reports are **expert evidence** under the Spanish [Civil Procedure Act (LEC)](/en/court-appointed-expert/); the difference lies in who appoints and how independence is weighed.\n\n## How court appointment works\n\n1. The party requests in its pleadings that a court expert be appointed, or the judge orders it.\n2. The court turns to the **lists** submitted each year by the professional associations.\n3. The relevant specialist is appointed by lot.\n4. The expert **accepts the office**, swears or promises to act objectively, and issues the report within the deadline.\n5. At trial, the expert **ratifies** the report and answers questions from the parties and the judge.\n\n## What the report is worth\n\nExpert evidence is weighed under the **rules of sound judgement** (art. 348 LEC): the judge is not automatically bound by the report, but one that is **traceable, grounded in regulations and well defended in court** carries decisive weight. That is the key: it is not enough to conclude — you must **show how the conclusion was reached** so the court can verify it.\n\n## What makes a report strong\n\n* Methodology documented at every stage (inspection, testing, analysis).\n* Regulatory grounding (CTE, LOE, EHE, Eurocodes) and the *lex artis*.\n* Traceability: dated photographs, measurements, chain of custody of samples.\n* The ability to **defend it orally** under cross-examination.\n\nA report that meets these requirements withstands the opposing party's [counter-report](/en/counter-report/).\n\n## For lawyers\n\nIf you are preparing a claim, involve the expert **as early as possible**: the choice of tests, the timing of the inspection and the drafting all shape the strength of the evidence. We work regularly with law firms through our [collaboration with lawyers and insurers](/en/lawyer-collaboration/).\n\n**Do you need an expert for your proceedings?** [Contact the firm](/en/contact/professional/)."
  },
  {
    "title": "How to challenge an opposing expert report: the counter-report",
    "slug": "challenge-opposing-expert-report",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/impugnar-informe-pericial-contrainforme/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/impugnar-informe-pericial-contrainforme/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/challenge-opposing-expert-report/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "What an expert counter-report is, the common flaws it detects in the opposing report, and how ratification is prepared to rebut it in court.",
    "excerpt": "If the opposing report contains errors, the counter-report exposes them technically. This is how an expert opinion is rebutted before the judge.",
    "estado": "programado",
    "datePublished": "2026-07-29",
    "dateModified": "2026-07-29",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell Serra",
    "category": "Legal",
    "tags": [
      "Counter-report",
      "Challenge",
      "Expert Evidence",
      "Ratification"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/informe-pericial-patologias-barcelona.jpg",
      "alt": "Two opposing expert reports on an office desk",
      "caption": "The counter-report reviews the methodology, data and regulations of the opposing report."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Expert Counter-Report",
        "url": "/en/counter-report/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## When the opposing expert report works against you\n\nIn many proceedings, the opposing party files its own expert report. If that report contains errors, omissions or forced conclusions, leaving it unanswered can cost the case. The technical tool to rebut it is the **expert counter-report**.\n\n## What a counter-report is\n\nA counter-report is an **expert opinion that critically reviews** the opposing party's report. It does not merely disagree: it analyses the methodology, checks the data and sets out, in technical terms, where it fails, so the judge can weigh both with proper criteria.\n\nA good counter-report answers three questions:\n\n1. **Is the methodology sound?** Were the right tests carried out, or was the conclusion drawn from a simple visual inspection?\n2. **Do the data support the conclusion?** Are there logical leaps, missing measurements or undated photographs?\n3. **Is the correct regulation applied?** A regulatory error (CTE, LOE, EHE) can invalidate the whole argument.\n\n## Common flaws in party reports\n\n* Conclusions **without supporting tests**.\n* Confused causes (for example, attributing to condensation what is infiltration).\n* Cost assessments **without a breakdown** or measurement criteria.\n* Lack of traceability: it is impossible to reconstruct how the conclusion was reached.\n* Overreach: the expert opines outside their specialty.\n\nDetecting and **documenting** these points is what turns disagreement into evidence.\n\n## The ratification moment\n\nThe counter-report is completed at the **hearing**, when both experts appear. Rigorous preparation makes it possible to put precise technical questions to the opposing expert that expose the weaknesses of their report before the judge. This **oral defence** stage is as important as the written document.\n\n## A full counter-report is not always needed\n\nSometimes a **technical review** is enough to help the lawyer prepare cross-examination; other times, a full alternative report is warranted. The decision depends on the strength of the opposing report and what is at stake. We assess it case by case within our [counter-report](/en/counter-report/) service and our [collaboration with lawyers](/en/lawyer-collaboration/).\n\n**Facing an expert report you are not convinced by?** [Send it to us](/en/contact/professional/) and we will tell you whether it can be rebutted, and how."
  },
  {
    "title": "Roof leaks in a shared building: who to claim from",
    "slug": "roof-leaks-homeowners-association-claim",
    "translations": [
      {
        "lang": "es",
        "permalink": "/blog/filtraciones-cubierta-comunidad-reclamar/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "ca",
        "permalink": "/ca/blog/filtracions-coberta-comunitat-reclamar/"
      },
      {
        "lang": "en",
        "permalink": "/en/blog/roof-leaks-homeowners-association-claim/"
      }
    ],
    "description": "Common or private element, how the real origin of a roof leak is located, and the process to claim from the association, the builder or the insurer.",
    "excerpt": "Water does not appear where it gets in. Who pays for a roof leak depends on the origin: here is how it is determined and claimed.",
    "estado": "programado",
    "datePublished": "2026-08-05",
    "dateModified": "2026-08-05",
    "author": "Albert Vilardell Serra",
    "category": "Pathologies",
    "tags": [
      "Leaks",
      "Roof",
      "Owners' Association",
      "Claim"
    ],
    "image": {
      "url": "/assets/images/deteccion-humedades-termografia.jpg",
      "alt": "Waterproofing test on the roof of a residential building",
      "caption": "The waterproofing test locates the real point where water enters the roof."
    },
    "relatedServices": [
      {
        "title": "Damp and Leaks",
        "url": "/en/expert-witness-reports/damp-leaks/"
      }
    ],
    "content": "## Water on the top floor: who pays for the repair?\n\nLeaks through a building's **roof** are one of the most frequent sources of conflict in owners' associations. Water gets in through the roof terrace, shows up on the ceiling of the penthouse or top floor, and the argument begins over who should pay for the repair. The answer depends on **where the origin is**, and that requires a technical diagnosis.\n\n## Common or private element: the key to liability\n\nAs a general rule:\n\n* If the leak comes from the **communal roof or terrace**, the general downpipes or the building's waterproofing, the repair falls to the **association** (common element).\n* If it comes from a **privately used terrace**, a private installation or works by the owner, liability may be **individual**.\n\nThe nuance matters greatly for **level terraces** or privately used roofs: they are for private use, but the **structural and waterproofing element** is usually common. That is where most disputes arise, and where a report is decisive.\n\n## Why the origin is not obvious\n\nWater **does not appear where it gets in**. It can seep through one point of the roof and travel several metres under the paving or between slabs before showing up as a stain. That is why our [damp and leak report](/en/expert-witness-reports/damp-leaks/) locates the real entry point with waterproofing tests and thermography, instead of repairing blindly.\n\n## The process to make a claim\n\n1. **Document** the leak from the outset (dated photos, notice to the association).\n2. Request an **expert report** that determines the origin and establishes whether it is a common or private element.\n3. With the report, the association (or the affected owner) can **claim from the right party**: the building's insurer, the builder if within the warranty period ([LOE](/en/poor-execution-claim/)), or another owner.\n4. If there is no agreement, the report serves as **evidence** in court.\n\n## The role of the association's insurance\n\nMany association policies cover water damage but **dispute the origin** to avoid paying. An independent report proving the cause keeps the claim from getting stuck between the association, the owner and the insurer.\n\n**Leaks in your building and no one taking responsibility?** [Tell us about it](/en/contact/professional/) and we will determine the origin and the liability."
  }
